{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016LNjBDNR2UshEERxCznzVa","type":"tool_result","content":"300\t\n301\t**Orientation.** Home plate H is in the south and center field in the north (−y); x runs east, so first base is on the\n302\tright of the screen. Spray angle θ is measured from straight-away center, + toward right field. Fair territory is\n303\t|θ| ≤ 45°.\n304\t\n305\t| Point | Offset from H (x, y) in U |\n306\t|---|---|\n307\t| plate front edge (the strike-zone plane) | (0, −6) |\n308\t| first base | (+212, −212) |\n309\t| second base | (0, −424) |\n310\t| third base | (−212, −212) |\n311\t| pitching rubber | (0, −205) |\n312\t| mound | circle r 26 at (0, −200), drawn raised 3 U (a shape; play stays at z 0) |\n313\t| batter's boxes | centers (−18, −4) for righties and (+18, −4) for lefties, 12 × 22 |\n314\t| catcher | (0, +16), crouched |\n315\t| umpire | (0, +30) |\n316\t| on-deck circles | 40 U outside each foul line, 70 U from H along it: about (−78, −21) and (+78, −21) |\n317\t| dugouts | set into each foul wall, from 110 to 230 U along the line from H, 30 U deep, with benches and a low roof (cut-down walls) |\n318\t\n319\t**Fielders' default spots:** P (0, −200) · C (0, +16) · 1B (+225, −275) · 2B (+105, −395) · SS (−105, −395) ·\n320\t3B (−225, −275) · LF (−420, −760) · CF (0, −900) · RF (+420, −760). A park can shift its outfielders back by its\n321\tdepth.\n322\t\n323\t**Fence.** `r = R(θ)`: a Catmull-Rom curve over θ through five control distances (LF line, LC gap, CF, RC gap, RF line;\n324\tper park in §7.2). The fence height `Hf(θ)` defaults to 40 U, with optional sections (the MegaDome's 90-U Mega Wall).\n325\tFoul poles stand at θ = ±45°, 150 U tall. **Foul walls** (the stands' front) run parallel to the foul lines, 70 U\n326\toutside them, from each pole in toward home until they are 110 U from H (at about (−110, −10) and (+110, −10)). The\n327\t**backstop** is the arc r = 110 around H that joins them behind the plate (it passes (0, +110)). Check any new\n328\tcoordinate against this outline: everything on the field must be inside it.\n329\t\n330\t**Surfaces.**\n331\t- Infield dirt: r ≤ 470 and |θ| ≤ 50°, minus the infield grass square (inside the basepaths, inset 22 U); plus 22-U\n332\t  basepath strips, the home circle (r 44) and the mound.\n333\t- Warning track: dirt from R(θ) − 48 to R(θ).\n334\t- Chalk: foul lines 2 U wide to the fence; batter's and catcher's boxes; coaches' boxes; on-deck circles.\n335\t- Grass: mowing pattern per park (stripes, checkerboard or diamonds), using `E.hash2` and `E.noise2` grain as the\n336\t  sports kit does.\n337\t- Beyond the fence: the park's own ground (cornfield, sea, lava, regolith, graves, neon concourse).\n338\t\n339\t**The TileMap is generated** by the park builder from these functions, one cell per 16 U (about 120 × 105 cells for a\n340\tpark with a 1,180-U center field). Floor tags: `grass`, `dirt`, `track`, `foul`, `out`, and per park `water`, `lava`,\n341\t`regolith`, `grave`, `concourse`. Wall types:\n342\t\n343\tLegend characters map to numeric wall type ids (`types: { 1: {...} }`), as in the sports kit:\n344\t\n345\t| Legend char | Use | Settings |\n346\t|---|---|---|\n347\t| `F` | the fence | h = Hf, padded material per park, `roof: 'plain'` in the park's top-line color |\n348\t| `1`–`4` | stand tiers, front to back | h 52 / 64 / 76 / 88; face `'stone'`, `'plank'` or a park material |\n349\t| `B` | the backstop | h 64, net look |\n350\t| `D` | dugouts | h 20, `cut: true` |\n351\t\n352\tEverything south of the plate is `cut: true`, since it faces the camera. `floorTex` paints the lines and patterns. The\n353\tPlate Cam's Mode 7 floor is the same TileMap (`new E.Mode7({ floor: map, sky: park.backdrop })`).\n354\t\n355\t**Who collides with what.** The ball uses the analytic geometry (R(θ), wall segments, poles), not tiles. Players are\n356\t`E.Body`s that collide with the TileMap walls. The crowd sits on the stand tiles. Props (light towers, scoreboards,\n357\tpark features) are `r.shape` entries; solid ones are added with `map.solid`.\n358\t\n359\t---\n360\t\n361\t## 6. Systems\n362\t\n363\tEach system lists what it does, its numbers (starting values in `TUNE`, tuned in play) and its tests (IDs refer to §11.3).\n364\t\n365\t### 6.1 Match flow and the rules engine (`10-rules.js`, `30-match.js`)\n366\t\n367\t**State `BZ.M`:** `mode` ('tour' | 'exhibition' | 'training' | 'attract' | 'derby') · `park` · `teams[0]` = away,\n368\t`teams[1]` = home, each `{ def, lineup[9], order, pitcher, bullpen[], runs, hits, errors, meter, turbo, line[] }` ·\n369\t`inning`, `half` ('top' | 'bottom'), `outs`, `balls`, `strikes` · `bases[3]` (runner id or null) · `phase` · `ball` ·\n370\t`fielders[9]` · `runners[]` · `batter`, `pitcher`, `catcher`, `umpire` · `control` (which human runs which job, §6.20) ·\n371\t`stats` (per player: AB, H, 2B, 3B, HR, RBI, BB, K, SB; pitcher: IP, K, BB, pitches) · `seed` · `play` (the live play's\n372\town record: contact, who touched the ball, outs made, runs scored).\n373\t\n374\t**Phases:** `pregame` (lineup cards, \"PLAY BALL!\") → `halfStart` (\"TOP 1ST\" card; fielders jog out) → `atBat`\n375\t(`walkUp` → `pitchSelect` → `windup` → `pitchFlight`) → `inPlay` (ball live) → `playOver` (calls, runs, replay) → the\n376\tnext `atBat` or `halfEnd` (\"SIDE RETIRED\", line score, a between-innings show) → … → `final` (results). Side states:\n377\t`cinematic`, `replay`, `rhubarb`, `between`.\n378\t\n379\t**Rules kept:** balls and strikes; four balls is a walk; three strikes is a strikeout; a foul is a strike except with\n380\ttwo strikes, but a foul bunt with two strikes is a strikeout; hit by pitch sends the batter to first; fair and foul by\n381\twhere the ball lands or is first touched (past the bags) or where it stops or is touched (before them); caught flies\n382\tare outs, fair or foul; tag-ups after a catch; force outs and tag outs; runs count when the runner touches home before\n383\tthe third out, unless the third out is a force or the batter-runner is out before first; home runs (a fair fly over the\n384\tfence, or off the pole); ground-rule doubles (a fair bounce over the fence); inside-the-park home runs; errors (a dropped\n385\tcatch, a wild throw); stolen bases and pickoffs; wild pitches and passed balls; double plays (and triple plays when they\n386\thappen); walk-offs; the home team skips the bottom of the last inning when it leads.\n387\t\n388\t**Simplified:** extra innings start each half with a runner on second (the **Bonus Runner**). After 3 extra innings the\n389\tgame goes to **SUDDEN BOOM**, a Home Run Duel that reuses the Derby rules (§6.18): each side's best slugger takes 5\n390\tswings against the Pitch-O-Matic, and the most home-run feet wins (a tie goes on one swing each). The **mercy rule** ends a game at a 10-run lead after 2 full\n391\tinnings (a setting, on by default). Innings per game are 1, 3, 5 or 9 (Exhibition setting; Tour: 3, or 1 in Quick).\n392\t\n393\t**Dropped**, and listed on Spring Training's \"House Rules\" card as what you can't do: balks, the infield fly rule,\n394\tappeals, interference and obstruction, check swings (every swing is committed), dropped third strikes, intentional\n395\twalks, substitutions other than a pitching change, and arguing with the umpire (\"believe us, we tried\").\n396\t\n397\t**Play over:** the play ends when (a) the ball is dead (home run, foul, caught third out, ground-rule double), or (b) a\n398\tfielder holds the ball inside the infield dirt (r < 470) and no runner has moved toward a base for 0.6 s, or (c) the\n399\tpitcher holds it on the mound. A runner left between bases when (b) begins is played out: the AI fielder with the ball\n400\tchases them (a simple rundown, §6.5) and they go back or on.\n401\t\n402\t**Tests:** T-RULES-01..24 (count, walks, strikeouts, foul with two strikes, foul bunt, HBP force chain, force and tag\n403\touts, the run-scoring timing on the third out, tag-ups, ground-rule double, home run with runners, walk-off, skipping the\n404\tbottom of the last inning, extra innings with the Bonus Runner, SUDDEN BOOM, mercy rule, line score and box stats).\n405\t\n406\t### 6.2 Pitching (`11-pitch.js`)\n407\t\n408\t**Repertoire:** each pitcher has five pitches, one per button. Cross is the heater, Circle a breaking ball (curve or\n409\tslider), Square an off-speed pitch (changeup, sinker or knuckler), Triangle their signature pitch, and R1 their team's\n410\tsuper pitch (when the meter is full). Catalog in §7.3.\n411\t\n412\t**Delivery, for a human:**\n413\t1. **Pick:** press a pitch button (keyboard 1–5, or K / L / J / I / E; mouse: the wheel picks, the left button\n414\t   throws). The\n415\t   pitcher comes set and the windup starts.\n416\t2. **Aim:** during the windup (0.9 s × the pitcher's tempo), the stick, WASD or mouse moves the **target marker** over\n417\t   the zone plane, out to 8 U beyond its edges.\n418\t3. **Release:** a **release ring** closes on the marker over the last 0.45 s of the windup. Press the same pitch button\n419\t   (or the left mouse button) when it meets the marker's inner circle. Release quality q ∈ [0, 1]: perfect within ±40 ms\n420\t   (× `TUNE.releaseWindow`), linear down to 0 at ±200 ms; no press means an auto-release with q = 0.3. q adds 0–8% speed\n421\t   and sharpens the break by 0–20%. The miss radius around the target is lerp(5, 0.5, q) U × fatigue.\n422\t4. **Aftertouch:** for the first 40% of the flight, the stick bends the ball up to ±4 U across and ±3 U up or down,\n423\t   × the pitcher's BRK factor × `TUNE.aftertouch`. It costs 0.5 stamina.\n424\t\n425\t**The CPU pitcher** (§6.11) picks from a count-and-batter table, aims at edges with an error by difficulty, rolls q from\n426\tits difficulty, and uses aftertouch at higher levels.\n427\t\n428\t**Flight model.** Parametric and exact, so the plate crossing is known at release. With u = t / T, where T = distance\n429\t/ speed:\n430\t- `pos(u) = lerp(release, target + miss, u) + break(u) + after(u)`\n431\t- `z(u)` adds a sag of `4 · u · (1 − u) · sag`, so the pitch arcs\n432\t- `break(u) = [bx, bz] · u^k`, where k is the pitch's lateness exponent\n433\t\n434\tSpecial paths (zig-zag, vanish, wobble, wave) are functions of u (§7.3). The heater's base speed is 340 U/s\n435\t(88 mph; flight about 0.57 s over the 193 U from release to the plate), × the pitch's factor × the pitcher's VEL factor\n436\t(§7) × (1 + 0.08 · q) × the stamina factor × `TUNE.pitchSpeed`.\n437\t\n438\t**Calls.** At the plate plane the ball's (x, z) is checked against the batter's zone, widened by the ball's radius (1.6 U).\n439\tThe zone is 12 U wide (x −6..+6), from the knees (z 9 × size/1.25) to the chest (z 25 × size/1.25). A pitch in the zone\n440\tthat isn't swung at is a strike, outside it a ball. The ball's crossing point shows as a dot for 1.5 s.\n441\t\n442\t**Stamina:** 100 at the start. A pitch costs heater 1.2, breaking 1.6, off-speed 1.0, signature 2.2, super 5 (×\n443\t`TUNE.staminaDrain`). Below 40: speed ×0.92, miss ×1.4. Below 15 (\"gassed\"): ×0.85 and ×2. The CPU calls the bullpen\n444\tbelow 20; a human sees a \"BULLPEN'S READY\" toast and can use Pitching Change in the pause menu. Each team has one\n445\treliever.\n446\t\n447\t**Catcher:** catches every pitch that crosses inside x ±16, z 0..34. A ball outside that, or in the dirt (z < 2), is a\n448\twild pitch or passed ball with chance 0.35 × (1 − catcher's GLV / 10), and the ball rolls loose to the backstop.\n449\t\n450\t**Pickoffs:** before picking a pitch, L1 plus a base button throws over (the CPU does it rarely). A runner leading off\n451\tmore than 20 U is tagged out if the throw beats them back.\n452\t\n453\t**Tests:** T-PITCH-01..12 (each pitch's crossing equals the planned target within the miss radius; flight time; break\n454\tsize and lateness; aftertouch bounded; release quality → speed and miss; stamina drain and fatigue; calls at the zone\n455\tedges; wild pitch chance; the super pitches' special paths; pickoff timing).\n456\t\n457\t### 6.3 Batting and the contact model (`12-bat.js`)\n458\t\n459\t**Reticle (the sweet spot).** A circle on the zone plane that the stick or WASD moves (80 U/s, eased) within the zone\n460\t±6 U; the mouse places it directly (pointer → zone plane by the zone's projected corners, §6.14). Its radius is\n461\tr = 4.2 U for a contact swing and 2.8 U for a power swing, × the batter's CON factor (§7: 0.88–1.15) × `TUNE.sweetSpot` ×\n462\tthe difficulty's assist. It is drawn as a ring with a dot at its center, and a faint outer ring at 1.6 r (foul-tip range).\n463\t\n464\t**Swings:**\n465\t\n466\t| Button | Swing | Delay to the plate plane | Radius | Max exit speed |\n467\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n468\t| ✕ / K / Space / left click | Contact | 0.10 s | 4.2 | 720 U/s |\n469\t| □ / J / right click | Power | 0.14 s | 2.8 | 860 U/s |\n470\t| R1 / E, then a swing button | Batzooka (meter full; arms the next swing) | 0.16 s | 6.3 | 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6) ≈ 1,250 U/s, plus a rocket boost |\n471\t| △ / I, held | Bunt (square around; let go to pull back) | none: contact when the ball crosses inside the bunt radius 6 | 6 | 160 U/s |\n472\t\n473\t**Contact.** For a swing pressed at ts, the timing error is `e = ts + delay − tCross` (+ is late). The swing misses if\n474\t|e| > the OK window (90 ms × `TUNE.swingTiming` × assist) or if the distance from the ball to the reticle center is\n475\td > 1.6 r. The timing grades are **PERFECT** within ±22 ms, **GOOD** within ±50 ms and **OK** within ±90 ms (all ×\n476\t`TUNE.swingTiming` × assist). Otherwise:\n477\t- `dn = d / r`. When 1 < dn ≤ 1.6 the swing is a **foul tip**: the ball goes back or out of play (foul), or a weak\n478\t  dribbler (0.3 chance).\n479\t- `timing = 1 − (|e| / window)^1.5`, `Q = (1 − min(dn, 1)) · timing` (0..1).\n480\t- **Spray:** `θ = (e / window) · 48° + ((bx − rx) / r) · 10° + noise(±4°)` for a right-handed batter: early (e < 0)\n481\t  pulls to left field (θ < 0), late goes the other way, and an outside pitch (bx > rx) drifts toward right field.\n482\t  Negate the timing term for lefties (early pulls to right field). |θ| > 45° is foul.\n483\t- **Launch:** `φ = 12° − ((rz − bz) / r) · 35°`. Swinging above the ball (the reticle higher than the ball) drives a\n484\t  grounder; swinging under it lofts a fly. A power swing adds +6°; a Batzooka swing clamps φ to 28°–38°.\n485\t- **Exit speed:** `EV = lerp(220, EVmax, Q^1.3) × POW factor (0.88–1.15) × (0.9 + 0.1 · pitchSpeed / 340) ×\n486\t  TUNE.exitVelocity × park.evMul`.\n487\t- **Velocity:** `vx = EV cos φ sin θ`, `vy = −EV cos φ cos θ`, `vz = EV sin φ`. Sidespin has magnitude 3 · |e| (e in\n488\t  seconds) and curves the ball further toward the side it was hit: late swings slice to the opposite field, early ones\n489\t  hook to the pull side.\n490\t- **Feedback** (on for Rookie, Training and the \"Timing feedback\" setting): \"EARLY 34 ms\", \"PERFECT!\", \"LATE 12 ms\",\n491\t  \"UNDER IT\", \"TOPPED\".\n492\t\n493\t**Whiffs:** a swing and miss is a strike. A swing with no ball in reach (too early) still counts as a strike when the\n494\tpitch crosses.\n495\t\n496\t**Batter's box:** righties stand in the box on the third-base side and lefties on the first-base side. Lefties use the\n497\tmirrored mocap swing.\n498\t\n499\t**Tests:** T-BAT-01..14 (perfect center → line drive up the middle; early → pull, late → opposite field; reticle high →\n500\tgrounder, low → fly; outside 1.6 r → miss, between r and 1.6 r → foul tip; power vs contact radius and exit speed;\n501\tBatzooka forced loft; bunt exit; foul beyond 45°; lefty mirror; the assist multipliers; the reticle clamp; mouse\n502\tmapping).\n503\t\n504\t### 6.4 Ball physics (`13-ball.js`)\n505\t\n506\t**Integration** at 120 Hz: `v += (g + drag + spin + wind) · dt`, `p += v · dt`.\n507\t- g = (0, 0, −520 × park.gravityMul × `TUNE.gravity`)\n508\t- drag = −0.05 · v (`TUNE.drag`)\n509\t- spin = sidespin × speed × 0.004, across the path\n510\t- wind = (wx, wy) × windEffect(z), where windEffect is 0 below 60 U and 1 above 160\n511\t\n512\t**Ground:** at z ≤ 0 the ball bounces: `vz = −vz · e` (grass 0.42, dirt 0.50, track 0.45, regolith 0.55; ×\n513\t`TUNE.bounce`), and the speed along the ground ×0.82 on grass and ×0.90 on dirt. Once |vz| < 25 it rolls, slowing by\n514\t260 U/s² on grass and 180 on dirt (× `TUNE.rollFriction`) until it stops.\n515\t\n516\t**Fence:** a fair ball with r > R(θ) − 1.6:\n517\t- z ≥ Hf(θ) and the ball hasn't touched the ground → **HOME RUN**;\n518\t- z ≥ Hf(θ) after a bounce → **GROUND-RULE DOUBLE**;\n519\t- otherwise it bounces off the fence: the radial velocity reverses × 0.35 and the tangential part × 0.8.\n520\t\n521\t**Poles:** a ball within 3 U of a pole below 150 U is a home run (\"off the foul pole!\"). **Foul walls and backstop:**\n522\tsegment collisions with reflection × 0.4. A ball over them is out of play (foul), drawn flying into the crowd.\n523\t\n524\t**Prediction** (`BZ.ball.predict(ball, secs)`): forward simulation with the same integrator and no randomness. It gives\n525\tthe first landing point and time, the moments when the ball comes down through catch heights (z ≤ 34 descending), and\n526\tsamples along the path every 1/30 s for grounder intercepts. Fielding AI, the landing marker and the cameras use it.\n527\t\n528\t**Thrown balls:** `E.arc(from, to, g, { time })`, with the flight time from distance / throw speed and an apex of at\n529\tleast 6 U. A throw is caught when it passes within the receiver's catch radius at z 0..40. Off-target (error) throws\n530\tland 30–60 U wide.\n531\t\n532\t**Display height:** in the Broadcast Cam the ball and its trail are drawn at `z' = z < 40 ? z : 40 + (z − 40) · 0.45`\n533\t(`TUNE.heightSquash`), so high flies stay near their shadows on screen. Catching happens at glove heights, where z' = z.\n534\tThe true shadow and the landing marker (a pulsing X with a ring) are always drawn.\n535\t\n536\t**Tests:** T-BALL-01..12 (range of a 30° launch vs the closed form within 5% before drag; drag lowers range; bounce and\n537\troll stop; fence bounce vs home run vs ground-rule double; poles; foul walls; prediction equals the simulation\n538\texactly; wind; moon gravity; the display squash keeps z' = z at glove height).\n539\t\n540\t### 6.5 Fielding (`14-field.js`)\n541\t\n542\t**Assignment at contact.** For each fielder, compute the earliest **intercept**: for a fly, reaching the predicted\n543\tlanding point (or a catch-height crossing) before the ball; for a grounder, the first sampled path point where the\n544\tfielder's time to get there (reaction + distance / speed) is no more than the ball's. The fielder with the smallest\n545\tintercept time is the **chaser**. If the defense is human, the chaser is the controlled fielder, and L1 / Q switches\n546\tto the next best.\n547\t\n548\t**Everyone else covers by role:**\n549\t- 1B covers first; if 1B is the chaser, P covers it.\n550\t- 2B covers second on balls to the left side, SS on balls to the right side.\n551\t- 3B covers third; C covers home.\n552\t- P backs up home or third on hits to the outfield.\n553\t- The outfielder nearest the play backs it up.\n554\t- On a ball to the deep outfield, the SS (left side) or 2B (right side) goes out as the **cutoff man**, halfway to\n555\t  the outfielder.\n556\t\n557\t**Moving:** fielders are `E.Body` at 132 U/s × SPD factor × `TUNE.fielderSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35 (§6.8). CPU reaction\n558\ttime comes from the difficulty (§6.11); a human's control starts at once.\n559\t\n560\t**Catching:** a catch is attempted when the ball is within the catch radius (9 U × GLV factor × `TUNE.catchRadius`\n561\tacross the ground) and the ball's z is in the glove range: 0..34 standing, up to 70 at a jump's top, or up to Hf + 30\n562\tin a wall climb. Success:\n563\t`p = clamp(0.985 + 0.006 · (GLV − 5) − speedPenalty − 0.08 if diving − difficultyPenalty, 0.5, 0.995)`, where the speed\n564\tpenalty is 0.05–0.15 for balls above 600 U/s.\n565\tA failed catch is an **error**: the ball pops loose (vz 80, random direction from `rng.play`), with a \"clank\" and a\n566\tdizzy-stars gag.\n567\t\n568\t**Throwing:**\n569\t- A human presses a base button: ○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home (keyboard 1, 2, 3, 4, or L, I, J, K; mouse:\n570\t  click the base in the world, §6.14). Hold to power up: a 0.6 s meter on the fielder; let go to throw. Throw speed is 560 U/s × ARM factor\n571\t  (0.88–1.15) × (0.85 + 0.35 · power) × `TUNE.throwSpeed`.\n572\t- Above 85% power the throw can go wild: `p = (power − 0.85) × 0.8`, plus fatigue.\n573\t- With a cutoff man up, a throw to a base far away goes through him; he relays automatically (CPU), or the human\n574\t  presses the base again.\n575\t- **CPU throws** (and the \"Auto throw\" assist): for each runner in danger, lead runner first, compare the runner's time\n576\t  to the next base with the throw's time. Throw where the out is likely and worth the most; with no likely out, throw to\n577\t  the base ahead of the lead runner. After a force at second, the pivot man goes on to first by himself (the double\n578\t  play).\n579\t\n580\t**Outs on the bases:**\n581\t- **Force out:** a fielder holding the ball touches the forced base (within 8 U) before the runner arrives.\n582\t- **Tag out:** a fielder holding the ball comes within 10 U of a runner who is off base. The tag is automatic, with a\n583\t  swipe animation.\n584\t- **Rundown:** the fielder with the ball runs at the runner; a second fielder covers the base the runner heads for;\n585\t  they throw when the runner turns.\n586\t\n587\t**Dive** (R2 / C, or Ctrl): lunges 22 U along the facing in 0.35 s, low (z 0..14), and stays prone for 0.6 s.\n588\t**Jump** (R1 / E without the ball): a jump (vz 260, × park.jumpMul), with the glove up to 70. **Wall climb**: R1 within\n589\t18 U of the fence while the ball is coming high climbs the fence face (z up to Hf + 26) to **rob a home run**.\n590\t\n591\t**Assists (setting \"Fielding\")**: *Manual* (you run and throw); *Assisted* (the default: the chaser runs to the ball by\n592\titself, you throw); *Auto* (the CPU fields and throws, and you can still take over by moving the stick).\n593\t\n594\t**Tests:** T-FIELD-01..16 (the chaser on routine flies and grounders; cover roles by situation; catch radius and glove\n595\trange; jump and wall-climb ranges; a robbed home run counts as an out; dive; error rates in bounds over 1,000 seeded\n596\ttries; a throw's time; the force at first beats a slow runner on a routine grounder; double play 6-4-3; tag out off the\n597\tbase; rundown resolves; cutoff relay; assisted mode reaches the ball; mouse click-to-throw picks the base).\n598\t\n599\t### 6.6 Baserunning (`15-run.js`)\n600\t\n601\t**Runners** move along the basepath segments at 128 U/s × SPD factor (0.88–1.15) × `TUNE.runSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35. A\n602\trunner has a base (0 = the batter), a target, a progress, a state (`onBase`, `lead`, `running`, `returning`, `safe`,\n603\t`out`, `scored`) and the flags `forced` and `mustTagUp`.\n604\t- The **batter-runner** drops the bat and runs to first 0.25 s after contact, and may overrun first safely.\n605\t- **Forced** runners must advance. **Unforced** runners follow the AI, or the human's orders.\n606\t- On a **fly ball**, runners go halfway (AI) until it is caught or drops. After a catch they must go back and touch\n607\t  (tag up) before advancing.\n608\t\n609\t**Orders (human):**\n610\t- R1 / E: advance all runners one base.\n611\t- L1 / Q: send all back.\n612\t- Hold a base button plus R1 or L1: only the runner heading to that base (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home;\n613\t  keyboard 1–4, or L, I, J, K).\n614\t- Mouse: left-click a base to send the runner who can reach it; right-click a base to send that runner back.\n615\t\n616\t**Steals and leads:**\n617\t- Before the pitch, R1 / E takes an extra lead (+12 U; easier pickoffs).\n618\t- Hold L1 / Q and press the target base's button to **steal**: the runner breaks on the windup.\n619\t- The catcher throws (AI) to the base, and it's a tag race.\n620\t- The CPU runners steal by speed vs the catcher's arm and the pitch type.\n621\t\n622\t**Slides:**\n623\t- Within 28 U of a base on a close play, runners slide feet-first automatically (mocap `Slide_Start`, `Slide_Loop`,\n624\t  `Slide_Exit`), with dust.\n625\t- ✕ / K in the last 40 U is a **headfirst dive**: +10% speed over the last 20 U, more dust, and a hype bonus.\n626\t\n627\t**Bowl-over:** a runner on turbo who reaches home while the catcher holds the ball for a tag rolls POW + 1d6\n628\t(`rng.play`) against the catcher's POW + 1d6 + 1. If the runner wins, the ball pops loose and he's safe; if the\n629\tcatcher wins, he's out and bounces off. Either way it's \"COLLISION!\": a dust burst, stars, crowd \"OOOH\".\n630\t\n631\t**Scoring:** a runner who touches home (within 6 U) scores, subject to the third-out rule (§6.1).\n632\t\n633\t**Assists (setting \"Baserunning\")**: *Manual* or *Auto* (the CPU runs your runners; you can still give orders).\n634\t\n635\t**Tests:** T-RUN-01..14 (forced advance on a walk with the bases loaded; the batter-runner overruns first safely;\n636\ttag-up on a deep fly scores from third; leaving early on a caught fly means doubled off; advance all and retreat all;\n637\tan individual order; a steal succeeds with a fast runner and is thrown out with a slow one; extra lead raises the\n638\tpickoff risk; the slide triggers; the headfirst bonus; bowl-over both ways; turbo speed; the mouse orders).\n639\t\n640\t### 6.7 The Batzooka meter and supers (`16-supers.js`)\n641\t\n642\t**The meter** (per team, 0..100) is the crowd's roar. The jumbotron, the crowd's animation and a music layer all\n643\tfollow it. Gains, × `TUNE.meterGain` × the Comeback Boost (×1.5 for a team trailing by 3 or more, a setting):\n644\t\n645\t| Event | Gain |\n646\t|---|---|\n647\t| contact | +6 to +15 by Q |\n648\t| single | +10 |\n649\t| extra-base hit | +15 |\n650\t| home run | +25 |\n651\t| strikeout (to the pitcher's team) | +12 |\n652\t| diving catch or wall catch | +20 |\n653\t| double play | +15 |\n654\t| stolen base | +10 |\n655\t| headfirst slide safe | +5 |\n656\t| winning a Rhubarb | +50 |\n657\t| a park target hit | +20 |\n658\t\n659\tAt 100: \"BATZOOKA READY!\", a jumbotron flash, the crowd on its feet, and the music's \"hype\" layer comes in.\n660\t\n661\t**Supers** (what they cost from the meter):\n662\t\n663\t| Super | Who | Cost | Effect |\n664\t|---|---|---|---|\n665\t| **BATZOOKA!** | batter: R1 / E arms it, then any swing | 100 | The bat transforms (glowing barrel, nozzle and fins drawn over the bat); the reticle is 1.5× larger and on fire; on contact EVmax = 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6, about 1,250 U/s), loft 28–38°, a rocket boost along v of 900 U/s² for 0.5 s, a smoke and fire trail, the Moonshot Cam. Miss and it's \"FIZZLE!\": a comic smoke puff, and the meter is spent. |\n666\t| **Team super pitch** | pitcher: R1 / E at pitch select | 100 | The team's super path and effect (§7.3); 1.35× speed |\n667\t| **Laser Arm** | fielder holding the ball: R1 plus a base button | 50 | A flat, nearly instant throw (1,400 U/s) with a laser trail, never wild |\n668\t| **Afterburners** | runners: R1 plus L2 while running | 50 | All runners at ×1.8 for 3 s, with fire trails behind their feet |\n669\t\n670\tThe CPU uses supers when its meter is full and the moment is right: a Batzooka with runners on, or behind in the late\n671\tinnings; a super pitch with two strikes.\n672\t\n673\t**Tests:** T-SUPER-01..10 (gains per event; the comeback multiplier; arming and spending; the Batzooka loft and boost;\n674\tfizzle; each super pitch's path; Laser Arm speed and accuracy; Afterburners duration; the CPU's use conditions; the\n675\tmeter clamps at 100).\n676\t\n677\t### 6.8 Turbo and athletic moves\n678\t\n679\t**Turbo** (per team, 0..100) is held on L2 / Shift by runners or the controlled fielder. It drains 40/s and refills\n680\t10/s when not in use, plus 15 per pitch (× `TUNE.turboDrain`, `TUNE.turboPower`). The CPU uses it on close plays.\n681\tThe dive, jump, wall climb, slides and bowl-over are specified in §6.5 and §6.6. Every athletic move has its own\n682\tanimation (§8.3), a sound and a particle beat.\n683\t\n684\t### 6.9 The Rhubarb: the bench-clearing brawl (`17-rhubarb.js`)\n685\t\n686\t**Triggers:**\n687\t- a hit-by-pitch at 0.9 × the heater's base speed or faster, with chance `TUNE.rhubarbChance` (0.4);\n688\t- a bowl-over (0.25);\n689\t- the Tour's scripted moment at Caldera Field;\n690\t- the dev trigger.\n691\t\n692\tSetting \"Rhubarbs: Off\" turns them off; an HBP then just awards the base.\n693\t\n694\t**Flow:**\n695\t1. \"RHUBARB!\" stamp; the Broadcast Cam frames the plate area.\n696\t2. Six per side pour out of the dugouts (the batter, the on-deck hitter and the nearest teammates against the pitcher,\n697\t   the catcher and nearby fielders).\n698\t3. 12 seconds of brawling on the dirt around home. Each human controls one fighter (the batter, or the pitcher for the\n699\t   defense); the rest are AI."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37baaf64-6acc-44ac-b38d-1fce3a552962","uuid":"f1f7a234-339a-48da-b32d-eb5735f69eda","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:00:26.211Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-060015__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n**Orientation.** Home plate H is in the south and center field in the north (−y); x runs east, so first base is on the\nright of the screen. Spray angle θ is measured from straight-away center, + toward right field. Fair territory is\n|θ| ≤ 45°.\n\n| Point | Offset from H (x, y) in U |\n|---|---|\n| plate front edge (the strike-zone plane) | (0, −6) |\n| first base | (+212, −212) |\n| second base | (0, −424) |\n| third base | (−212, −212) |\n| pitching rubber | (0, −205) |\n| mound | circle r 26 at (0, −200), drawn raised 3 U (a shape; play stays at z 0) |\n| batter's boxes | centers (−18, −4) for righties and (+18, −4) for lefties, 12 × 22 |\n| catcher | (0, +16), crouched |\n| umpire | (0, +30) |\n| on-deck circles | 40 U outside each foul line, 70 U from H along it: about (−78, −21) and (+78, −21) |\n| dugouts | set into each foul wall, from 110 to 230 U along the line from H, 30 U deep, with benches and a low roof (cut-down walls) |\n\n**Fielders' default spots:** P (0, −200) · C (0, +16) · 1B (+225, −275) · 2B (+105, −395) · SS (−105, −395) ·\n3B (−225, −275) · LF (−420, −760) · CF (0, −900) · RF (+420, −760). A park can shift its outfielders back by its\ndepth.\n\n**Fence.** `r = R(θ)`: a Catmull-Rom curve over θ through five control distances (LF line, LC gap, CF, RC gap, RF line;\nper park in §7.2). The fence height `Hf(θ)` defaults to 40 U, with optional sections (the MegaDome's 90-U Mega Wall).\nFoul poles stand at θ = ±45°, 150 U tall. **Foul walls** (the stands' front) run parallel to the foul lines, 70 U\noutside them, from each pole in toward home until they are 110 U from H (at about (−110, −10) and (+110, −10)). The\n**backstop** is the arc r = 110 around H that joins them behind the plate (it passes (0, +110)). Check any new\ncoordinate against this outline: everything on the field must be inside it.\n\n**Surfaces.**\n- Infield dirt: r ≤ 470 and |θ| ≤ 50°, minus the infield grass square (inside the basepaths, inset 22 U); plus 22-U\n  basepath strips, the home circle (r 44) and the mound.\n- Warning track: dirt from R(θ) − 48 to R(θ).\n- Chalk: foul lines 2 U wide to the fence; batter's and catcher's boxes; coaches' boxes; on-deck circles.\n- Grass: mowing pattern per park (stripes, checkerboard or diamonds), using `E.hash2` and `E.noise2` grain as the\n  sports kit does.\n- Beyond the fence: the park's own ground (cornfield, sea, lava, regolith, graves, neon concourse).\n\n**The TileMap is generated** by the park builder from these functions, one cell per 16 U (about 120 × 105 cells for a\npark with a 1,180-U center field). Floor tags: `grass`, `dirt`, `track`, `foul`, `out`, and per park `water`, `lava`,\n`regolith`, `grave`, `concourse`. Wall types:\n\nLegend characters map to numeric wall type ids (`types: { 1: {...} }`), as in the sports kit:\n\n| Legend char | Use | Settings |\n|---|---|---|\n| `F` | the fence | h = Hf, padded material per park, `roof: 'plain'` in the park's top-line color |\n| `1`–`4` | stand tiers, front to back | h 52 / 64 / 76 / 88; face `'stone'`, `'plank'` or a park material |\n| `B` | the backstop | h 64, net look |\n| `D` | dugouts | h 20, `cut: true` |\n\nEverything south of the plate is `cut: true`, since it faces the camera. `floorTex` paints the lines and patterns. The\nPlate Cam's Mode 7 floor is the same TileMap (`new E.Mode7({ floor: map, sky: park.backdrop })`).\n\n**Who collides with what.** The ball uses the analytic geometry (R(θ), wall segments, poles), not tiles. Players are\n`E.Body`s that collide with the TileMap walls. The crowd sits on the stand tiles. Props (light towers, scoreboards,\npark features) are `r.shape` entries; solid ones are added with `map.solid`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Systems\n\nEach system lists what it does, its numbers (starting values in `TUNE`, tuned in play) and its tests (IDs refer to §11.3).\n\n### 6.1 Match flow and the rules engine (`10-rules.js`, `30-match.js`)\n\n**State `BZ.M`:** `mode` ('tour' | 'exhibition' | 'training' | 'attract' | 'derby') · `park` · `teams[0]` = away,\n`teams[1]` = home, each `{ def, lineup[9], order, pitcher, bullpen[], runs, hits, errors, meter, turbo, line[] }` ·\n`inning`, `half` ('top' | 'bottom'), `outs`, `balls`, `strikes` · `bases[3]` (runner id or null) · `phase` · `ball` ·\n`fielders[9]` · `runners[]` · `batter`, `pitcher`, `catcher`, `umpire` · `control` (which human runs which job, §6.20) ·\n`stats` (per player: AB, H, 2B, 3B, HR, RBI, BB, K, SB; pitcher: IP, K, BB, pitches) · `seed` · `play` (the live play's\nown record: contact, who touched the ball, outs made, runs scored).\n\n**Phases:** `pregame` (lineup cards, \"PLAY BALL!\") → `halfStart` (\"TOP 1ST\" card; fielders jog out) → `atBat`\n(`walkUp` → `pitchSelect` → `windup` → `pitchFlight`) → `inPlay` (ball live) → `playOver` (calls, runs, replay) → the\nnext `atBat` or `halfEnd` (\"SIDE RETIRED\", line score, a between-innings show) → … → `final` (results). Side states:\n`cinematic`, `replay`, `rhubarb`, `between`.\n\n**Rules kept:** balls and strikes; four balls is a walk; three strikes is a strikeout; a foul is a strike except with\ntwo strikes, but a foul bunt with two strikes is a strikeout; hit by pitch sends the batter to first; fair and foul by\nwhere the ball lands or is first touched (past the bags) or where it stops or is touched (before them); caught flies\nare outs, fair or foul; tag-ups after a catch; force outs and tag outs; runs count when the runner touches home before\nthe third out, unless the third out is a force or the batter-runner is out before first; home runs (a fair fly over the\nfence, or off the pole); ground-rule doubles (a fair bounce over the fence); inside-the-park home runs; errors (a dropped\ncatch, a wild throw); stolen bases and pickoffs; wild pitches and passed balls; double plays (and triple plays when they\nhappen); walk-offs; the home team skips the bottom of the last inning when it leads.\n\n**Simplified:** extra innings start each half with a runner on second (the **Bonus Runner**). After 3 extra innings the\ngame goes to **SUDDEN BOOM**, a Home Run Duel that reuses the Derby rules (§6.18): each side's best slugger takes 5\nswings against the Pitch-O-Matic, and the most home-run feet wins (a tie goes on one swing each). The **mercy rule** ends a game at a 10-run lead after 2 full\ninnings (a setting, on by default). Innings per game are 1, 3, 5 or 9 (Exhibition setting; Tour: 3, or 1 in Quick).\n\n**Dropped**, and listed on Spring Training's \"House Rules\" card as what you can't do: balks, the infield fly rule,\nappeals, interference and obstruction, check swings (every swing is committed), dropped third strikes, intentional\nwalks, substitutions other than a pitching change, and arguing with the umpire (\"believe us, we tried\").\n\n**Play over:** the play ends when (a) the ball is dead (home run, foul, caught third out, ground-rule double), or (b) a\nfielder holds the ball inside the infield dirt (r < 470) and no runner has moved toward a base for 0.6 s, or (c) the\npitcher holds it on the mound. A runner left between bases when (b) begins is played out: the AI fielder with the ball\nchases them (a simple rundown, §6.5) and they go back or on.\n\n**Tests:** T-RULES-01..24 (count, walks, strikeouts, foul with two strikes, foul bunt, HBP force chain, force and tag\nouts, the run-scoring timing on the third out, tag-ups, ground-rule double, home run with runners, walk-off, skipping the\nbottom of the last inning, extra innings with the Bonus Runner, SUDDEN BOOM, mercy rule, line score and box stats).\n\n### 6.2 Pitching (`11-pitch.js`)\n\n**Repertoire:** each pitcher has five pitches, one per button. Cross is the heater, Circle a breaking ball (curve or\nslider), Square an off-speed pitch (changeup, sinker or knuckler), Triangle their signature pitch, and R1 their team's\nsuper pitch (when the meter is full). Catalog in §7.3.\n\n**Delivery, for a human:**\n1. **Pick:** press a pitch button (keyboard 1–5, or K / L / J / I / E; mouse: the wheel picks, the left button\n   throws). The\n   pitcher comes set and the windup starts.\n2. **Aim:** during the windup (0.9 s × the pitcher's tempo), the stick, WASD or mouse moves the **target marker** over\n   the zone plane, out to 8 U beyond its edges.\n3. **Release:** a **release ring** closes on the marker over the last 0.45 s of the windup. Press the same pitch button\n   (or the left mouse button) when it meets the marker's inner circle. Release quality q ∈ [0, 1]: perfect within ±40 ms\n   (× `TUNE.releaseWindow`), linear down to 0 at ±200 ms; no press means an auto-release with q = 0.3. q adds 0–8% speed\n   and sharpens the break by 0–20%. The miss radius around the target is lerp(5, 0.5, q) U × fatigue.\n4. **Aftertouch:** for the first 40% of the flight, the stick bends the ball up to ±4 U across and ±3 U up or down,\n   × the pitcher's BRK factor × `TUNE.aftertouch`. It costs 0.5 stamina.\n\n**The CPU pitcher** (§6.11) picks from a count-and-batter table, aims at edges with an error by difficulty, rolls q from\nits difficulty, and uses aftertouch at higher levels.\n\n**Flight model.** Parametric and exact, so the plate crossing is known at release. With u = t / T, where T = distance\n/ speed:\n- `pos(u) = lerp(release, target + miss, u) + break(u) + after(u)`\n- `z(u)` adds a sag of `4 · u · (1 − u) · sag`, so the pitch arcs\n- `break(u) = [bx, bz] · u^k`, where k is the pitch's lateness exponent\n\nSpecial paths (zig-zag, vanish, wobble, wave) are functions of u (§7.3). The heater's base speed is 340 U/s\n(88 mph; flight about 0.57 s over the 193 U from release to the plate), × the pitch's factor × the pitcher's VEL factor\n(§7) × (1 + 0.08 · q) × the stamina factor × `TUNE.pitchSpeed`.\n\n**Calls.** At the plate plane the ball's (x, z) is checked against the batter's zone, widened by the ball's radius (1.6 U).\nThe zone is 12 U wide (x −6..+6), from the knees (z 9 × size/1.25) to the chest (z 25 × size/1.25). A pitch in the zone\nthat isn't swung at is a strike, outside it a ball. The ball's crossing point shows as a dot for 1.5 s.\n\n**Stamina:** 100 at the start. A pitch costs heater 1.2, breaking 1.6, off-speed 1.0, signature 2.2, super 5 (×\n`TUNE.staminaDrain`). Below 40: speed ×0.92, miss ×1.4. Below 15 (\"gassed\"): ×0.85 and ×2. The CPU calls the bullpen\nbelow 20; a human sees a \"BULLPEN'S READY\" toast and can use Pitching Change in the pause menu. Each team has one\nreliever.\n\n**Catcher:** catches every pitch that crosses inside x ±16, z 0..34. A ball outside that, or in the dirt (z < 2), is a\nwild pitch or passed ball with chance 0.35 × (1 − catcher's GLV / 10), and the ball rolls loose to the backstop.\n\n**Pickoffs:** before picking a pitch, L1 plus a base button throws over (the CPU does it rarely). A runner leading off\nmore than 20 U is tagged out if the throw beats them back.\n\n**Tests:** T-PITCH-01..12 (each pitch's crossing equals the planned target within the miss radius; flight time; break\nsize and lateness; aftertouch bounded; release quality → speed and miss; stamina drain and fatigue; calls at the zone\nedges; wild pitch chance; the super pitches' special paths; pickoff timing).\n\n### 6.3 Batting and the contact model (`12-bat.js`)\n\n**Reticle (the sweet spot).** A circle on the zone plane that the stick or WASD moves (80 U/s, eased) within the zone\n±6 U; the mouse places it directly (pointer → zone plane by the zone's projected corners, §6.14). Its radius is\nr = 4.2 U for a contact swing and 2.8 U for a power swing, × the batter's CON factor (§7: 0.88–1.15) × `TUNE.sweetSpot` ×\nthe difficulty's assist. It is drawn as a ring with a dot at its center, and a faint outer ring at 1.6 r (foul-tip range).\n\n**Swings:**\n\n| Button | Swing | Delay to the plate plane | Radius | Max exit speed |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ✕ / K / Space / left click | Contact | 0.10 s | 4.2 | 720 U/s |\n| □ / J / right click | Power | 0.14 s | 2.8 | 860 U/s |\n| R1 / E, then a swing button | Batzooka (meter full; arms the next swing) | 0.16 s | 6.3 | 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6) ≈ 1,250 U/s, plus a rocket boost |\n| △ / I, held | Bunt (square around; let go to pull back) | none: contact when the ball crosses inside the bunt radius 6 | 6 | 160 U/s |\n\n**Contact.** For a swing pressed at ts, the timing error is `e = ts + delay − tCross` (+ is late). The swing misses if\n|e| > the OK window (90 ms × `TUNE.swingTiming` × assist) or if the distance from the ball to the reticle center is\nd > 1.6 r. The timing grades are **PERFECT** within ±22 ms, **GOOD** within ±50 ms and **OK** within ±90 ms (all ×\n`TUNE.swingTiming` × assist). Otherwise:\n- `dn = d / r`. When 1 < dn ≤ 1.6 the swing is a **foul tip**: the ball goes back or out of play (foul), or a weak\n  dribbler (0.3 chance).\n- `timing = 1 − (|e| / window)^1.5`, `Q = (1 − min(dn, 1)) · timing` (0..1).\n- **Spray:** `θ = (e / window) · 48° + ((bx − rx) / r) · 10° + noise(±4°)` for a right-handed batter: early (e < 0)\n  pulls to left field (θ < 0), late goes the other way, and an outside pitch (bx > rx) drifts toward right field.\n  Negate the timing term for lefties (early pulls to right field). |θ| > 45° is foul.\n- **Launch:** `φ = 12° − ((rz − bz) / r) · 35°`. Swinging above the ball (the reticle higher than the ball) drives a\n  grounder; swinging under it lofts a fly. A power swing adds +6°; a Batzooka swing clamps φ to 28°–38°.\n- **Exit speed:** `EV = lerp(220, EVmax, Q^1.3) × POW factor (0.88–1.15) × (0.9 + 0.1 · pitchSpeed / 340) ×\n  TUNE.exitVelocity × park.evMul`.\n- **Velocity:** `vx = EV cos φ sin θ`, `vy = −EV cos φ cos θ`, `vz = EV sin φ`. Sidespin has magnitude 3 · |e| (e in\n  seconds) and curves the ball further toward the side it was hit: late swings slice to the opposite field, early ones\n  hook to the pull side.\n- **Feedback** (on for Rookie, Training and the \"Timing feedback\" setting): \"EARLY 34 ms\", \"PERFECT!\", \"LATE 12 ms\",\n  \"UNDER IT\", \"TOPPED\".\n\n**Whiffs:** a swing and miss is a strike. A swing with no ball in reach (too early) still counts as a strike when the\npitch crosses.\n\n**Batter's box:** righties stand in the box on the third-base side and lefties on the first-base side. Lefties use the\nmirrored mocap swing.\n\n**Tests:** T-BAT-01..14 (perfect center → line drive up the middle; early → pull, late → opposite field; reticle high →\ngrounder, low → fly; outside 1.6 r → miss, between r and 1.6 r → foul tip; power vs contact radius and exit speed;\nBatzooka forced loft; bunt exit; foul beyond 45°; lefty mirror; the assist multipliers; the reticle clamp; mouse\nmapping).\n\n### 6.4 Ball physics (`13-ball.js`)\n\n**Integration** at 120 Hz: `v += (g + drag + spin + wind) · dt`, `p += v · dt`.\n- g = (0, 0, −520 × park.gravityMul × `TUNE.gravity`)\n- drag = −0.05 · v (`TUNE.drag`)\n- spin = sidespin × speed × 0.004, across the path\n- wind = (wx, wy) × windEffect(z), where windEffect is 0 below 60 U and 1 above 160\n\n**Ground:** at z ≤ 0 the ball bounces: `vz = −vz · e` (grass 0.42, dirt 0.50, track 0.45, regolith 0.55; ×\n`TUNE.bounce`), and the speed along the ground ×0.82 on grass and ×0.90 on dirt. Once |vz| < 25 it rolls, slowing by\n260 U/s² on grass and 180 on dirt (× `TUNE.rollFriction`) until it stops.\n\n**Fence:** a fair ball with r > R(θ) − 1.6:\n- z ≥ Hf(θ) and the ball hasn't touched the ground → **HOME RUN**;\n- z ≥ Hf(θ) after a bounce → **GROUND-RULE DOUBLE**;\n- otherwise it bounces off the fence: the radial velocity reverses × 0.35 and the tangential part × 0.8.\n\n**Poles:** a ball within 3 U of a pole below 150 U is a home run (\"off the foul pole!\"). **Foul walls and backstop:**\nsegment collisions with reflection × 0.4. A ball over them is out of play (foul), drawn flying into the crowd.\n\n**Prediction** (`BZ.ball.predict(ball, secs)`): forward simulation with the same integrator and no randomness. It gives\nthe first landing point and time, the moments when the ball comes down through catch heights (z ≤ 34 descending), and\nsamples along the path every 1/30 s for grounder intercepts. Fielding AI, the landing marker and the cameras use it.\n\n**Thrown balls:** `E.arc(from, to, g, { time })`, with the flight time from distance / throw speed and an apex of at\nleast 6 U. A throw is caught when it passes within the receiver's catch radius at z 0..40. Off-target (error) throws\nland 30–60 U wide.\n\n**Display height:** in the Broadcast Cam the ball and its trail are drawn at `z' = z < 40 ? z : 40 + (z − 40) · 0.45`\n(`TUNE.heightSquash`), so high flies stay near their shadows on screen. Catching happens at glove heights, where z' = z.\nThe true shadow and the landing marker (a pulsing X with a ring) are always drawn.\n\n**Tests:** T-BALL-01..12 (range of a 30° launch vs the closed form within 5% before drag; drag lowers range; bounce and\nroll stop; fence bounce vs home run vs ground-rule double; poles; foul walls; prediction equals the simulation\nexactly; wind; moon gravity; the display squash keeps z' = z at glove height).\n\n### 6.5 Fielding (`14-field.js`)\n\n**Assignment at contact.** For each fielder, compute the earliest **intercept**: for a fly, reaching the predicted\nlanding point (or a catch-height crossing) before the ball; for a grounder, the first sampled path point where the\nfielder's time to get there (reaction + distance / speed) is no more than the ball's. The fielder with the smallest\nintercept time is the **chaser**. If the defense is human, the chaser is the controlled fielder, and L1 / Q switches\nto the next best.\n\n**Everyone else covers by role:**\n- 1B covers first; if 1B is the chaser, P covers it.\n- 2B covers second on balls to the left side, SS on balls to the right side.\n- 3B covers third; C covers home.\n- P backs up home or third on hits to the outfield.\n- The outfielder nearest the play backs it up.\n- On a ball to the deep outfield, the SS (left side) or 2B (right side) goes out as the **cutoff man**, halfway to\n  the outfielder.\n\n**Moving:** fielders are `E.Body` at 132 U/s × SPD factor × `TUNE.fielderSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35 (§6.8). CPU reaction\ntime comes from the difficulty (§6.11); a human's control starts at once.\n\n**Catching:** a catch is attempted when the ball is within the catch radius (9 U × GLV factor × `TUNE.catchRadius`\nacross the ground) and the ball's z is in the glove range: 0..34 standing, up to 70 at a jump's top, or up to Hf + 30\nin a wall climb. Success:\n`p = clamp(0.985 + 0.006 · (GLV − 5) − speedPenalty − 0.08 if diving − difficultyPenalty, 0.5, 0.995)`, where the speed\npenalty is 0.05–0.15 for balls above 600 U/s.\nA failed catch is an **error**: the ball pops loose (vz 80, random direction from `rng.play`), with a \"clank\" and a\ndizzy-stars gag.\n\n**Throwing:**\n- A human presses a base button: ○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home (keyboard 1, 2, 3, 4, or L, I, J, K; mouse:\n  click the base in the world, §6.14). Hold to power up: a 0.6 s meter on the fielder; let go to throw. Throw speed is 560 U/s × ARM factor\n  (0.88–1.15) × (0.85 + 0.35 · power) × `TUNE.throwSpeed`.\n- Above 85% power the throw can go wild: `p = (power − 0.85) × 0.8`, plus fatigue.\n- With a cutoff man up, a throw to a base far away goes through him; he relays automatically (CPU), or the human\n  presses the base again.\n- **CPU throws** (and the \"Auto throw\" assist): for each runner in danger, lead runner first, compare the runner's time\n  to the next base with the throw's time. Throw where the out is likely and worth the most; with no likely out, throw to\n  the base ahead of the lead runner. After a force at second, the pivot man goes on to first by himself (the double\n  play).\n\n**Outs on the bases:**\n- **Force out:** a fielder holding the ball touches the forced base (within 8 U) before the runner arrives.\n- **Tag out:** a fielder holding the ball comes within 10 U of a runner who is off base. The tag is automatic, with a\n  swipe animation.\n- **Rundown:** the fielder with the ball runs at the runner; a second fielder covers the base the runner heads for;\n  they throw when the runner turns.\n\n**Dive** (R2 / C, or Ctrl): lunges 22 U along the facing in 0.35 s, low (z 0..14), and stays prone for 0.6 s.\n**Jump** (R1 / E without the ball): a jump (vz 260, × park.jumpMul), with the glove up to 70. **Wall climb**: R1 within\n18 U of the fence while the ball is coming high climbs the fence face (z up to Hf + 26) to **rob a home run**.\n\n**Assists (setting \"Fielding\")**: *Manual* (you run and throw); *Assisted* (the default: the chaser runs to the ball by\nitself, you throw); *Auto* (the CPU fields and throws, and you can still take over by moving the stick).\n\n**Tests:** T-FIELD-01..16 (the chaser on routine flies and grounders; cover roles by situation; catch radius and glove\nrange; jump and wall-climb ranges; a robbed home run counts as an out; dive; error rates in bounds over 1,000 seeded\ntries; a throw's time; the force at first beats a slow runner on a routine grounder; double play 6-4-3; tag out off the\nbase; rundown resolves; cutoff relay; assisted mode reaches the ball; mouse click-to-throw picks the base).\n\n### 6.6 Baserunning (`15-run.js`)\n\n**Runners** move along the basepath segments at 128 U/s × SPD factor (0.88–1.15) × `TUNE.runSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35. A\nrunner has a base (0 = the batter), a target, a progress, a state (`onBase`, `lead`, `running`, `returning`, `safe`,\n`out`, `scored`) and the flags `forced` and `mustTagUp`.\n- The **batter-runner** drops the bat and runs to first 0.25 s after contact, and may overrun first safely.\n- **Forced** runners must advance. **Unforced** runners follow the AI, or the human's orders.\n- On a **fly ball**, runners go halfway (AI) until it is caught or drops. After a catch they must go back and touch\n  (tag up) before advancing.\n\n**Orders (human):**\n- R1 / E: advance all runners one base.\n- L1 / Q: send all back.\n- Hold a base button plus R1 or L1: only the runner heading to that base (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home;\n  keyboard 1–4, or L, I, J, K).\n- Mouse: left-click a base to send the runner who can reach it; right-click a base to send that runner back.\n\n**Steals and leads:**\n- Before the pitch, R1 / E takes an extra lead (+12 U; easier pickoffs).\n- Hold L1 / Q and press the target base's button to **steal**: the runner breaks on the windup.\n- The catcher throws (AI) to the base, and it's a tag race.\n- The CPU runners steal by speed vs the catcher's arm and the pitch type.\n\n**Slides:**\n- Within 28 U of a base on a close play, runners slide feet-first automatically (mocap `Slide_Start`, `Slide_Loop`,\n  `Slide_Exit`), with dust.\n- ✕ / K in the last 40 U is a **headfirst dive**: +10% speed over the last 20 U, more dust, and a hype bonus.\n\n**Bowl-over:** a runner on turbo who reaches home while the catcher holds the ball for a tag rolls POW + 1d6\n(`rng.play`) against the catcher's POW + 1d6 + 1. If the runner wins, the ball pops loose and he's safe; if the\ncatcher wins, he's out and bounces off. Either way it's \"COLLISION!\": a dust burst, stars, crowd \"OOOH\".\n\n**Scoring:** a runner who touches home (within 6 U) scores, subject to the third-out rule (§6.1).\n\n**Assists (setting \"Baserunning\")**: *Manual* or *Auto* (the CPU runs your runners; you can still give orders).\n\n**Tests:** T-RUN-01..14 (forced advance on a walk with the bases loaded; the batter-runner overruns first safely;\ntag-up on a deep fly scores from third; leaving early on a caught fly means doubled off; advance all and retreat all;\nan individual order; a steal succeeds with a fast runner and is thrown out with a slow one; extra lead raises the\npickoff risk; the slide triggers; the headfirst bonus; bowl-over both ways; turbo speed; the mouse orders).\n\n### 6.7 The Batzooka meter and supers (`16-supers.js`)\n\n**The meter** (per team, 0..100) is the crowd's roar. The jumbotron, the crowd's animation and a music layer all\nfollow it. Gains, × `TUNE.meterGain` × the Comeback Boost (×1.5 for a team trailing by 3 or more, a setting):\n\n| Event | Gain |\n|---|---|\n| contact | +6 to +15 by Q |\n| single | +10 |\n| extra-base hit | +15 |\n| home run | +25 |\n| strikeout (to the pitcher's team) | +12 |\n| diving catch or wall catch | +20 |\n| double play | +15 |\n| stolen base | +10 |\n| headfirst slide safe | +5 |\n| winning a Rhubarb | +50 |\n| a park target hit | +20 |\n\nAt 100: \"BATZOOKA READY!\", a jumbotron flash, the crowd on its feet, and the music's \"hype\" layer comes in.\n\n**Supers** (what they cost from the meter):\n\n| Super | Who | Cost | Effect |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **BATZOOKA!** | batter: R1 / E arms it, then any swing | 100 | The bat transforms (glowing barrel, nozzle and fins drawn over the bat); the reticle is 1.5× larger and on fire; on contact EVmax = 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6, about 1,250 U/s), loft 28–38°, a rocket boost along v of 900 U/s² for 0.5 s, a smoke and fire trail, the Moonshot Cam. Miss and it's \"FIZZLE!\": a comic smoke puff, and the meter is spent. |\n| **Team super pitch** | pitcher: R1 / E at pitch select | 100 | The team's super path and effect (§7.3); 1.35× speed |\n| **Laser Arm** | fielder holding the ball: R1 plus a base button | 50 | A flat, nearly instant throw (1,400 U/s) with a laser trail, never wild |\n| **Afterburners** | runners: R1 plus L2 while running | 50 | All runners at ×1.8 for 3 s, with fire trails behind their feet |\n\nThe CPU uses supers when its meter is full and the moment is right: a Batzooka with runners on, or behind in the late\ninnings; a super pitch with two strikes.\n\n**Tests:** T-SUPER-01..10 (gains per event; the comeback multiplier; arming and spending; the Batzooka loft and boost;\nfizzle; each super pitch's path; Laser Arm speed and accuracy; Afterburners duration; the CPU's use conditions; the\nmeter clamps at 100).\n\n### 6.8 Turbo and athletic moves\n\n**Turbo** (per team, 0..100) is held on L2 / Shift by runners or the controlled fielder. It drains 40/s and refills\n10/s when not in use, plus 15 per pitch (× `TUNE.turboDrain`, `TUNE.turboPower`). The CPU uses it on close plays.\nThe dive, jump, wall climb, slides and bowl-over are specified in §6.5 and §6.6. Every athletic move has its own\nanimation (§8.3), a sound and a particle beat.\n\n### 6.9 The Rhubarb: the bench-clearing brawl (`17-rhubarb.js`)\n\n**Triggers:**\n- a hit-by-pitch at 0.9 × the heater's base speed or faster, with chance `TUNE.rhubarbChance` (0.4);\n- a bowl-over (0.25);\n- the Tour's scripted moment at Caldera Field;\n- the dev trigger.\n\nSetting \"Rhubarbs: Off\" turns them off; an HBP then just awards the base.\n\n**Flow:**\n1. \"RHUBARB!\" stamp; the Broadcast Cam frames the plate area.\n2. Six per side pour out of the dugouts (the batter, the on-deck hitter and the nearest teammates against the pitcher,\n   the catcher and nearby fielders).\n3. 12 seconds of brawling on the dirt around home. Each human controls one fighter (the batter, or the pitcher for the\n   defense); the rest are AI.","numLines":400,"startLine":300,"totalLines":2144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016LNjBDNR2UshEERxCznzVa","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghvu2pkLdUKVc8j66qBL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F6LfXWGdnH8v4LeUTaJ7wd","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-060015__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","offset":700,"limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12732,"cache_read_input_tokens":33670,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12732},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37baaf64-6acc-44ac-b38d-1fce3a552962","uuid":"62e2ce60-1143-4343-92d4-080da96e2ae9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:00:28.180Z","request_id":"req_011Cghvu2WAGPeJfRFmFknXM","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01F6LfXWGdnH8v4LeUTaJ7wd":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-060015__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","offset":700,"limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F6LfXWGdnH8v4LeUTaJ7wd","type":"tool_result","content":"700\t4. The umpire's whistle: \"BREAK IT UP!\" Everyone dusts off and walks back.\n701\t5. The side with more knockdowns gets +50 meter; a tie gives both +20. A comic \"ejection\" line follows, though nobody\n702\t   is really ejected.\n703\t6. The batter takes first base for the HBP.\n704\t\n705\t**Fighting:**\n706\t- Stick: move. ✕ / K: punch combo (`E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook'])`). □ / J: shove (`'bash'`, `E.knockback` 160).\n707\t  △ / I: grab and toss (within 12 U: the `throw` move; the foe flies 40 U). ○ / L: dodge (a dash).\n708\t- Three hits knock a fighter down: pose `down`, dizzy stars for 2 s, then up again.\n709\t- AI fighters approach the nearest foe and throw combos with a 0.25 s wind-up so they telegraph.\n710\t- Fights between AI fighters with no human nearby are drawn as **fight clouds**: a smoke ball (`particles.smoke`) with\n711\t  fists, feet and a cap popping out.\n712\t\n713\t**Tone:** cartoon slapstick only: bonks, slide whistles, \"POW\" stamps, no blood.\n714\t\n715\t**Tests:** T-RHU-01..06 (trigger conditions and the setting; duration; scoring by knockdowns; meter award; the batter\n716\ton first after; no stray state after it ends).\n717\t\n718\t### 6.10 Hazards, park features and wind (`18-hazards.js`)\n719\t\n720\tEach park has one hazard or feature, as a plug-in `{ init(M), update(M, dt), onBall(M, ball), draw(r), hud() }`. Its\n721\trate is × `TUNE.hazardRate`. It is always **telegraphed** (a sound, a warning stamp, smoke) and introduced by a radio\n722\tline the first time it happens.\n723\t\n724\t| Park | Hazard / feature | Rule |\n725\t|---|---|---|\n726\t| Kernel Field | **Silo Shot** | A home run that hits the silo beyond right field pops it like popcorn: popcorn rains, +300 Fan Bucks, +20 meter. Corn stalks sway with the wind. |\n727\t| Barnacle Bay Park | **Gull Patrol** and **Splash Zone** | Gulls circle above 200 U. A fly ball with its apex above 200 has a 25% chance per gull pass of being snatched; the gull carries it 60–140 U, then drops it from z 150 (still in play). A home run to right field lands in the bay: a splash and a boat horn. |\n728\t| Caldera Field | **Lava Vents** | Two vents in the gaps (r 820, θ ±22°) erupt every 25 s (3 s of rumble and smoke first). A ball touching an erupting vent is launched (vz +600, a random push from `rng.play`); CPU fielders keep 30 U away and a human is knocked back. Ash falls; the outfield glows. |\n729\t| Tranquility Dome | **Low Gravity** and **Meteor Showers** | Gravity ×0.55, exit speed ×0.8, jumps ×2, deep fences. Every 40 s a meteor shower leaves 3 craters (decals) in the outfield for the rest of the half-inning; a grounder rolling through one turns by ±40°. |\n730\t| Boneyard Ballpark | **Phantom Fog** and **Ghost Gloves** | Fly balls above 120 U go invisible (only the shadow and the landing marker show) until they come back down. The Haunts' fielders reach +40 U in wall catches (they phase into the fence). |\n731\t| MegaMax MegaDome | **Defense Drones**, the **MEGA-TRON** and the **Boss** | Three drones patrol the outfield sky (z 150–250) on `E.Path`s. A fly ball that hits one deflects; a Batzooka hit destroys it (an explosion, +150 Fan Bucks). A ball off the MEGA-TRON jumbotron in center field is worth +300. The boss rules are in §7.1. |\n732\t\n733\t**Wind** (the four open-air parks; the two domes have none): set per game from `rng.play`: a direction and 0–12 U/s² of push on\n734\tballs above 60 U. A windsock (a shape) and the banners' sway show it, and a HUD arrow with mph. On the moon the radio jokes that there's no air.\n735\t\n736\t**Tests:** T-HAZ-01..08 (each hazard fires at its rate and has its effect on a scripted ball; telegraphs come before\n737\tthe event; the hazard rate slider; wind changes range in the right direction).\n738\t\n739\t### 6.11 CPU AI and difficulty (`20-ai.js`)\n740\t\n741\tFour levels: **ROOKIE, PRO, ALL-STAR, BATZOOKA**.\n742\t\n743\t| | Rookie | Pro | All-Star | Batzooka |\n744\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n745\t| CPU swing timing σ | 60 ms | 42 ms | 30 ms | 20 ms |\n746\t| CPU aim error σ | 3.6 U | 2.6 U | 1.8 U | 1.2 U |\n747\t| CPU discipline (takes a ball outside the zone) | 0.45 | 0.60 | 0.72 | 0.82 |\n748\t| CPU release quality (mean) | 0.50 | 0.68 | 0.80 | 0.90 |\n749\t| CPU aftertouch use | 0 | 0.3 | 0.6 | 0.9 |\n750\t| CPU fielder reaction | 0.34 s | 0.25 s | 0.19 s | 0.14 s |\n751\t| CPU pitch speed | ×0.85 | ×1.0 | ×1.07 | ×1.15 |\n752\t| Human sweet spot and timing window | ×1.25 | ×1.0 | ×1.0 | ×0.9 |\n753\t\n754\tThe CPU skills are also × `TUNE.cpuBat`, `cpuPitch`, `cpuField` and `cpuRun`.\n755\t\n756\t- **CPU batter:** after its reaction delay it predicts the crossing point with an aim error and moves the reticle\n757\t  there. It decides to swing from P(in zone) and its discipline, with a timing error from N(0, σ). It uses power swings\n758\t  in hitter's counts (2-0, 3-1) and when it is strong; bunts now and then with a runner on first and no outs; and a\n759\t  Batzooka when its meter is full.\n760\t- **CPU pitcher:** a pitch-mix table by count (ahead: break it off; behind: heater) with the batter's power in mind. It\n761\t  targets the corners, more as the level rises, and uses its super with two strikes when its meter is full.\n762\t- **CPU fielders:** §6.5. **CPU runners:** advance by the time race with a margin from the level, tag up when they can\n763\t  make it, steal by speed against the arm.\n764\t- **The bot** (`BZ.bot`): runs these brains for player 1's side and turns their choices into **input actions**\n765\t  (`input.hold`, `input.tap`, the reticle's target as stick moves). Tests, the scripted playthrough and the attract\n766\t  mode use it to play the real game through the real controls.\n767\t\n768\t**Tests:** T-AI-01..08 (CPU vs CPU over seeded 9-inning games at Pro (6 games in the default run, 30 with `--full`;\n769\tsims run with `M.fast = true`: no walk-ups, shows, replays or cinematics, and the CPU decides at once): runs per team per game between 3 and 9; K% of\n770\tplate appearances 12–32%; HR per plate appearance 2–8%; batting average .230–.330; errors per game 0.2–1.5; no game\n771\tover 40 minutes of game time; no stuck play, meaning every play ends within 20 s; harder levels win more against easier\n772\tones).\n773\t\n774\t### 6.12 Cameras, transitions and replays (`21-cams.js`)\n775\t\n776\t**Plate Cam** (Mode 7): `m7.cam = { x: H.x + side, y: H.y + 72, h: 36, angle: −π/2, pitch: 9, fov: 46 }` (`TUNE.plateDist`,\n777\t`plateHeight`, `plateFov`). `side` is 4 U toward the side away from the batter, so the zone is clear.\n778\t- The Plate Cam setting has two choices: *Classic* (the default) and *Close* (dist 52, h 30, fov 42).\n779\t- The sky is the park's `E.Backdrop`. The floor is the park TileMap.\n780\t- Billboards each frame: pitcher, fielders, batter, catcher, base runners and on-deck hitter with `m7.rig`; light\n781\t  towers and props with `m7.shape`; the fence, stand faces, crowd rows, foul poles and scoreboard with `m7.add` quads\n782\t  and sprites (far to near); the ball and its shadow with `m7.add` (radius max(1.5, 1.6 · k) px, stitches, the trail of\n783\t  a super).\n784\t- The umpire isn't drawn in the Plate Cam (he'd fill the screen); his calls show as a cut-in portrait.\n785\t- The catcher crouches below the zone, drawn at 85% opacity where he overlaps it.\n786\t- Overlays: the zone (a 3×3 grid; the setting is Always / During the pitch / Off), the reticle or target marker, the\n787\t  release ring, the crossing dot. These are drawn from `m7.project` of their world points.\n788\t- On contact the camera shakes for 0.15 s.\n789\t\n790\t**Plate Cam → Broadcast Cam.** On fair contact or a pop-up: hit-stop 0.06 s (`game.freeze`), a white flash\n791\t(`game.flash('#fff', .06)`), then a cut to the Broadcast Cam framing the plate. Within 0.3 s it takes up\n792\t`cam.frame([ballShadow, ballDrawn, landingMarker, chaser], { margin: 48, zoom: [.5, 1.1] })` with `cam.smooth = .15`.\n793\t- Fouls back over the screen stay in the Plate Cam (the ball shrinks up and away, then \"FOUL BALL\").\n794\t- Pop-ups high over the infield go to the Broadcast Cam, zoomed in.\n795\t\n796\t**Broadcast Cam framing by play:**\n797\t- the ball in the air: the ball, its marker and the chaser;\n798\t- a fielder with the ball: him, the throw's target base and the lead runner;\n799\t- runners only: the lead runner and the next base.\n800\t\n801\tZoom stays within 0.5–1.1 × `TUNE.fieldZoom`. A wide screen shows more of the park; an upright phone zooms in. After\n802\tthe play, a cut back to the Plate Cam for the next pitch.\n803\t\n804\t**Moonshot Cam.** When the prediction says a fair ball will clear the fence by 10 U or more (or on any Batzooka hit):\n805\t1. `game.timeScale = .4`; Mode 7 `m7.chase(ball, { back: 60, h: 20 })` looks along the flight over the fence;\n806\t2. the landing in the stands, a target or the bay: crowd scramble, fireworks, confetti (Broadcast Cam at the landing\n807\t   point);\n808\t3. normal speed; the **trot** in the Broadcast Cam (the batter jogs the bases, high-fiving the coaches; ×2 speed;\n809\t   skippable with ✕).\n810\t\n811\tThe whole sequence lasts about 4 s plus the trot.\n812\t\n813\t**Replays.** A ring buffer keeps the last 8 s of the world at 60 Hz: the ball, every body's x, y, z and facing, and each\n814\trig's update inputs. After a big play (home run, diving or wall catch, double play, bowl-over, Batzooka, robbed homer)\n815\tthe replay runs at ×0.5 in the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45, a new angle on the play), with a \"REPLAY\" banner and a pulsing\n816\tcorner, and the jumbotron shows it too. ✕ skips it. The \"Replays\" setting is On / Big plays only (default) / Off.\n817\t\n818\t**Cutscene cameras:** `game.tween` on a focus point and the zoom, run by the cutscene runner (§6.15).\n819\t\n820\t**Tests:** T-CAM-01..07 (the Plate Cam projects the zone fully on screen at 427×240 and 320×240 for both box sides;\n821\tthe cut on contact; the framing keeps ball and chaser on screen across 200 seeded batted balls (from snapshots of their\n822\tscreen positions); the Moonshot Cam trigger; the replay buffer replays positions exactly; skips work; timeScale is\n823\trestored).\n824\t\n825\t### 6.13 HUD, prompts and glyphs (`22-hud.js`, `01-input.js`)\n826\t\n827\t**Layout** for a TV at 10 feet, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H` with a 12-pixel safe margin (TV overscan), kept clear of\n828\t`game.cornerArea()`:\n829\t- **Scorebug**, top left (scale 1 text, the score at scale 2): AWAY and HOME abbreviations with runs; the inning with\n830\t  ▲ or ▼; two out pips; the count as **B ●●○ S ●○**; a mini diamond with lit bases.\n831\t- **Meters**, top right: the batting team's BATZOOKA gauge (a bat-shaped bar that fills with flame and blinks when\n832\t  full); the turbo bar; the wind arrow with mph.\n833\t- **Player card**, bottom left: a portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`), the name, one stat line (avg and HR this game) or the\n834\t  pitcher's stamina bar.\n835\t- **Prompts**, bottom center: 2–5 icon-and-verb pairs for the current context. An action not available now is greyed\n836\t  with its reason (\"BATZOOKA 63%\").\n837\t- **Radio box**: the announcers' lines with portraits, bottom left above the player card (2 lines, non-modal, auto\n838\t  advance).\n839\t- **Call stamps**, center top: STEE-RIKE!, BALL FOUR, FOUL BALL, SAFE!, YER OUT!, HOME RUN!, BATZOOKA!!!, DOUBLE PLAY!,\n840\t  RHUBARB!. Drawn with `E.font.title`, scale 3–4, a 3-color gradient, a pop-in tween and shake.\n841\t- **Pitch info** after each pitch: \"CURVE 71 MPH\" (supers show silly speeds: \"MEGA HEATER 131 MPH!!\").\n842\t- **Radar** in the Broadcast Cam, top right under the meters: the diamond and outfield with fielders, runners and\n843\t  the ball.\n844\t- **Pitching select**: four pitch names around a diamond of face-button icons in their positions (△ top, ○ right,\n845\t  ✕ bottom, □ left), plus \"R1 SUPER\"; the stamina bar. With the mouse: the wheel highlights a pitch, left click throws.\n846\t- **Throw targets** in the Broadcast Cam: when a fielder holds the ball, each base shows a ring with its button's icon\n847\t  (○ 1B, △ 2B, □ 3B, ✕ HOME). With the mouse the rings take hover (they grow) and clicks.\n848\t\n849\t**Rules:** the important information is at scale 2 or more (the 5×7 font at scale 1 is about 31 px tall on a 1080p\n850\tTV); no screen shows more than about 7 items of text at once; information is never in color alone (shapes and icons\n851\ttoo); every HUD piece hides during cutscenes and replays.\n852\t\n853\t**The glyph icon renderer** `BZ.glyph(g, label, x, y, size = 11)` draws the icon for the string `input.label(action)`\n854\treturns (the font cannot draw ✕ ○ □ △ in color):\n855\t- PlayStation: a dark disc (#1b1d26) with a light rim and the symbol in its classic color: ✕ blue #7aa7ff,\n856\t  ○ red #ff6b6b, □ pink #f08ad8, △ green #4fe0b0.\n857\t- Xbox: a dark disc with a colored letter: A green #6cc04a, B red #e8584a, X blue #4a8ef0, Y yellow #f4c430.\n858\t- Shoulders and triggers (L1, R1, L2, R2, LB, RB, LT, RT): rounded pills with the text.\n859\t- Options, Create, Menu, View: small pills. L3, R3, LS, RS: a stick ring with L or R.\n860\t- D-pad: a cross with the direction lit.\n861\t- Keyboard: a keycap (a light rounded rectangle with dark text; wide for Space, Shift, Enter).\n862\t- Mouse: a mouse outline with the left or right half filled, or the wheel lit.\n863\t\n864\t**The prompt builder** `BZ.prompts(ctx)` → `[[action, verb, enabled, why]]`, drawn as icon + verb.\n865\t- Labels follow `game.input.lastDevice`: for a gamepad, the `glyphs` family (PlayStation by default, Xbox by setting);\n866\t  for the keyboard, keys; for the mouse, the mouse buttons where the action has one and keys for the rest.\n867\t- Each player's prompts use their own input (`p.input.label`).\n868\t\n869\t**Tests:** T-HUD-01..08 (`label` → icon type for every action on each device and family; the setting switches the\n870\tfamily; prompts change with `lastDevice` within one step; nothing in the HUD overlaps the corner area or goes outside\n871\tthe safe margins at 320×240, 427×240 and 320×690; greyed prompts give a reason).\n872\t\n873\t### 6.14 Input and controls (`01-input.js`)\n874\t\n875\t**One preset**, positional, passed as `new E.Game({ input: BZ.KEYS })` and on every scene. Actions are named by the\n876\tbutton's position because the same button does a different job in each context (swing, fastball, throw home, punch).\n877\tThat also keeps rebinding conflict-free. On the keyboard, the number keys and PC-standard keys come first (below), and\n878\tthe I J K L cluster is an alternate set laid out like the face buttons.\n879\t\n880\t| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyph | Keyboard | Mouse |\n881\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n882\t| `south` | ✕ (Pad0) | A | Space, K | left button |\n883\t| `east` | ○ (Pad1) | B | L | – |\n884\t| `west` | □ (Pad2) | X | J | right button |\n885\t| `north` | △ (Pad3) | Y | I | – |\n886\t| `l1` | L1 (Pad4) | LB | Q, Tab | – |\n887\t| `r1` | R1 (Pad5) | RB | E | – |\n888\t| `l2` | L2 (Pad6) | LT | Left Shift | – |\n889\t| `r2` | R2 (Pad7) | RT | C, Left Ctrl | – |\n890\t| `view` | R3 (Pad11) | RS | V | – |\n891\t| `up/down/left/right` | left stick, D-pad | | W A S D, arrows | – |\n892\t| `confirm` | ✕ | A | Enter, Space, K | click a menu item |\n893\t| `cancel` | ○ | B | Escape, Backspace, L | – |\n894\t| `pause` / `start` | Options (Pad9) | Menu | Escape, P / Enter | – |\n895\t| the reticle and pitch target | left stick | | W A S D, arrows | the pointer (absolute) |\n896\t| `num1`–`num5` | (face buttons do these jobs) | | 1 2 3 4 5 | – |\n897\t| pitch type | face buttons | | 1–5 (or K L J I, E) | wheel to pick, left button to throw |\n898\t\n899\t**What each button does, by context** (prompts and the Playbook show this per device):\n900\t\n901\t| Context | ✕ south | ○ east | □ west | △ north | L1 | R1 | L2 | R2 | stick or mouse |\n902\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n903\t| Batting | contact swing | – | power swing | bunt (hold) | steal: hold, plus a base button | arm the BATZOOKA | – | – | aim the reticle |\n904\t| Pitching | heater | breaking ball | off-speed | signature | pickoff: hold, plus a base button | super pitch | – | – | aim; aftertouch in flight |\n905\t| Fielding (holding the ball) | throw home | throw to 1B | throw to 3B | throw to 2B | switch fielder | Laser Arm (plus a base button) | turbo | – | run; click a base to throw |\n906\t| Fielding (chasing) | – | – | – | – | switch fielder | jump / wall climb | turbo | dive | run |\n907\t| Running | headfirst dive (near a base) | (base select) | (base select) | (base select) | all back (or one, with a base button) | all advance (or one, with a base button) | turbo; with R1, Afterburners | – | mouse: left-click a base to send, right-click to bring back |\n908\t| Rhubarb | punch combo | dodge | shove | grab and toss | – | – | – | – | move |\n909\t| Menus | select | back | – | – | previous tab | next tab | – | – | hover focuses, click selects |\n910\t\n911\t- **The keyboard is PC-native first:** WASD and the mouse aim and run; Space swings and confirms; the mouse's left\n912\t  button swings, throws at a clicked base and selects; the right button is the power swing; **number keys pick**\n913\t  (`num1`–`num5`: 1–5 the pitch type when pitching, 1–4 the base, first to home, wherever a base is chosen); Shift\n914\t  is turbo (sprint); Tab switches fielder; E is the super and \"go\"; Q is the steal or \"back\" modifier; C or Ctrl\n915\t  dives; Escape pauses. The I J K L cluster stays as an alternate set that mirrors the face buttons, for players who\n916\t  like a pad layout on keys. Keyboard prompts show the number keys first.\n917\t- **Base buttons:** a face button picks a base by its position (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home) in three cases:\n918\t  when your fielder holds the ball (a throw), while L1 is held (a steal, a pickoff, sending one runner back), and while\n919\t  R1 is held (sending one runner, or a Laser Arm throw). Otherwise the face buttons do their usual jobs.\n920\t- **Mouse:** the pointer aims the bat or the pitch (pointer → zone plane by inverse-interpolating the zone's 4\n921\t  projected corners). In the Broadcast Cam, `game.mouseGround()` within 26 U of a base hovers it. Fielding and running\n922\t  use `input.suppressCode('Mouse0')` / `'Mouse2'` unless the click lands on a base, so a stray click never throws home.\n923\t  The wheel is the game's in a match (`camera: { zoom: false }` on the match scene).\n924\t- **Device tracking:** `lastDevice` switches on use; prompts follow it the next step. Moving the mouse counts as mouse\n925\t  use.\n926\t- **AI Hub rule:** in a session, Options is ignored while Create (Pad8) is held (the hub's bar).\n927\t- **Local players 2–4** get their own `game.players[i]` inputs: a gamepad each, or half a keyboard each\n928\t  (`E.Input.halfKeys`). The mouse belongs to player 1.\n929\t- **Rumble:** contact (strength by Q), catches, collisions, Batzooka (strong), Rhubarb hits (`input.rumble`; vibration\n930\t  setting).\n931\t\n932\t**Tests:** T-IN-01..10 (each context routes the right action; a press in a menu doesn't leak into play\n933\t(`consumeAll`); the mouse mapping onto the zone; a base click throws and a stray click doesn't; the wheel and the number keys\n934\tpick pitch types, and the number keys pick bases; Create plus Options in a session is ignored; half-keyboard players; rebinding a positional action changes all\n935\tof its contexts' prompts).\n936\t\n937\t### 6.15 Commentary, writing and babble voices (`23-talk.js`, `02-audio.js`)\n938\t\n939\t**The cast's voices:** HAP HARRIGAN (play-by-play; warm, quick, hyperbolic); \"BIG\" LOU BOMBECK (color; a retired\n940\tslugger with malapropisms and tall tales); GLORIA VOX (the PA; slow, echoing); UMPIRE BARB HAMMERSMITH (calls with\n941\tgusto); and in the Tour, the captains, GRANDPA IKE and MORTIMER MEGAMAX.\n942\t\n943\t**The commentary director:**\n944\t- It listens to the bus and picks a line from the pool for the event's tag (Appendix C, §7.9).\n945\t- Lines carry priority: 3 for a home run, Batzooka, Rhubarb, walk-off or robbery; 2 for outs, hits and steals; 1 for\n946\t  routine plays. A higher priority line interrupts.\n947\t- At most one line every 2.5 s. The same line isn't repeated within 20 lines.\n948\t- Variables: `{batter} {pitcher} {team} {dist} {mph} {park} {score}`.\n949\t- Hap and Lou alternate on big plays (Hap calls it, Lou reacts).\n950\t- Lines show in the radio box (`E.Dialog` with `modal: false`, `auto: 2.4`, portrait, alpha .85). The PA and the\n951\t  umpire show as stamps plus their own voice.\n952\t- The \"Commentary\" setting is On / Big plays only / Off.\n953\t\n954\t**Writing style.** It is the source's style: baseball radio broadcasting, turned up.\n955\t- Short punchy sentences in the present tense, and one image per line (\"That ball had a map and a flashlight\").\n956\t- Baseball slang: dinger, tater, moonshot, frozen rope, can of corn, chin music, painting the black, Uncle Charlie, the\n957\t  heater, cheese, seeing-eye single, worm-burner, Texas leaguer, ducks on the pond, the hot corner.\n958\t- Running gags: Lou's stories about 1979; the vendor who always sells out; the mascot's bad luck.\n959\t- Lines are no longer than about 90 characters.\n960\t- Never mean, no profanity, no real players or teams, and no real quotes.\n961\t\n962\t**Babble** (Animalese/Simlish-style voices, `BZ.babble`):\n963\t- **Hooks:** for dialog, set `talk.voice = null` and poll `talk.shown` each update; for each newly typed letter of\n964\t  `talk.pages[talk.i]`, play a syllable. Stamps and the PA call `BZ.babble.say(text, voice)` straight.\n965\t- **Syllables:** each letter a–z plays one note.\n966\t  - Pitch = base × 2^((map[letter] + contour) / 12). Vowels are a 0, e +4, i +7, o +2, u −3, y +5; consonants a small\n967\t    offset from a hash.\n968\t  - Length = 1 / rate (× 1.3 for vowels, × 0.8 for consonants).\n969\t  - Each note is `game.audio.sfx({ wave, freq, to: freq · 1.06, dur, vol, vib }, { vary: 0 })`. s, z and x add a\n970\t    10-ms noise burst.\n971\t- **Spaces and punctuation:** a space is a 40-ms gap; `.` `,` `!` `?` are longer pauses. After `!` the next 4 notes go\n972\t  +3 semitones; `?` rises at the end. A word in capitals is louder and slower.\n973\t- **Voice profiles:**\n974\t\n975\t| Voice | Wave | Base Hz | Rate (letters/s) | Other |\n976\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n977\t| Hap | pulse | 190 | 20 | range 7 |\n978\t| Lou | square | 120 | 15 | vib [6, .02] (gravel) |\n979\t| Gloria (PA) | triangle | 150 | 9 | an echo copy at +120 ms, 40% volume |\n980\t| Umpire Barb | square | 260 | 14 | long vowels held with vibrato (\"STEE-RIIIKE\") |\n981\t| Dot | pulse12 | 300 | 22 | |\n982\t| Ike | triangle | 170 | 12 | vib [5, .04] (shaky) |\n983\t| Mortimer | saw | 140 | 16 | nasal |\n984\t| ENFORCER-9000 | saw plus square | 90 | 18 | pitch quantized to a 3-note arpeggio |\n985\t| skeletons | triangle | 220 | 18 | noise \"clacks\" |\n986\t| other captains | | | | a distinct base and wave each (§7.1) |\n987\t\n988\t- **Volume:** the \"Voices\" slider (`settings.define`, 0–1, default 0.8).\n989\t- **Subtitles:** all speech is gibberish, so the text is always on screen.\n990\t\n991\t**Cutscene runner** `BZ.cut(steps)` runs a `game.script` over data steps:\n992\t`['say', who, text]`, `['walk', who, x, y]`, `['pose', who, pose, expr]`, `['face', who, angle]`, `['cam', x, y, zoom, secs]`,\n993\t`['sfx', name]`, `['music', song]`, `['fx', kind, x, y]`, `['wait', s]`, `['spawn', ...]`, `['call', fn]`.\n994\t✕ advances a line; holding ○ for 1 s skips the scene (a ring fills around the icon).\n995\t\n996\t**Tests:** T-TALK-01..07 (every event tag has at least 6 lines; the rate limit; priority interrupts; variables fill in;\n997\tno line over 90 characters; babble plays one syllable per letter, deterministically; the skip-hold works).\n998\t\n999\t### 6.16 Music and sound effects (`02-audio.js`, `56-songs.js`)\n1000\t\n1001\t**Music**, in the engine's song notation (`{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ name, wave, vol, notes, on }] }`). All original:\n1002\tno real anthems, and not even public-domain ballpark tunes. The ballpark **organ** is square + triangle an octave\n1003\tapart with a slow vibrato.\n1004\t\n1005\t| Song | Mood and makeup |\n1006\t|---|---|\n1007\t| `title` \"Batzooka!\" | 140 bpm; organ riff + chip rock bass + drums; a rocket-whoosh sting on the logo |\n1008\t| `menu` \"Clubhouse Shuffle\" | laid-back organ shuffle, 100 bpm |\n1009\t| `tourmap` \"Barnstorm Boogie\" | road boogie, 120 bpm |\n1010\t| one in-game loop per park | Kernel Field county-fair organ; Barnacle Bay 6/8 shanty; Caldera minor drums and saw bass; Tranquility spacey arpeggios; Boneyard spooky organ with a theremin-like vibrato sine; MegaDome synthwave. Ducked at the plate; layers named `base`, `hype` (the meter full), `drums` (runners in scoring position) |\n1011\t| `rally` | an original organ \"rally\" climb, played when the home team puts runners on |\n1012\t| `derby` | breakneck surf rock, 160 bpm |\n1013\t| `rhubarb` | a frantic chase, 180 bpm |\n1014\t| `training` | light and bouncy |\n1015\t| `ending` and `credits` | an anthemic reprise of the title |\n1016\t| `hubcard` | a title variation, quieter |\n1017\t\n1018\t**Stings** (`audio.sting`, on the bar): home run fanfare, strikeout sting, Batzooka ignition, inning change,\n1019\tvictory, defeat, between-innings show.\n1020\t\n1021\t**Sound effects** (`audio.define`; vary by quality):\n1022\t- **Bat:** \"tink\" (weak), \"crack\" (solid), \"KRAKOOM\" (Batzooka: noise burst + low boom + ring).\n1023\t- **Gloves:** mitt \"POP\", glove \"thup\".\n1024\t- **Pitches:** a whoosh pitched by speed; foul tip; thud in the dirt.\n1025\t- **Ball:** bounce, fence clang or thud, pole \"ping\".\n1026\t- **Crowd:** a filtered-noise murmur loop as a layer, swelling with the meter; cheer; groan; \"OOOH\"; boos; gasp.\n1027\t- **Ballpark:** organ stabs, the PA's echo.\n1028\t- **Players:** footsteps, slide, dive thump, climb scrabble, throw whoosh, collision crash.\n1029\t- **Spectacle:** fireworks (boom + crackle), the Batzooka charge (a rising saw and noise), a rocket trail loop, the\n1030\t  engine's `explode`.\n1031\t- **Parks:** gull caw, lava rumble, meteor whoosh, ghost \"boo\", drone buzz, boat horn.\n1032\t- **Interface:** meter-full ding, turbo whoosh, menu select, confirm and cancel.\n1033\t- **Rhubarb:** bonk, slide whistle, \"pow\".\n1034\t\n1035\t**Tests:** T-AUD-01..04 (every sound name used is defined; every scene sets its music; no unknown-song or unknown-sound\n1036\twarnings in `game.errors` or the console; the Voices setting scales babble).\n1037\t\n1038\t### 6.17 Crowd and ballpark life (`24-crowd.js`)\n1039\t\n1040\t- **Crowd seats** on the stand tiles: 2–3 people a tile, drawn as in the sports kit: little bodies in shirts of the home\n1041\t  and away colors, skin tones, caps. Over 1,500 in a park, drawn `r.queue` with `every: 2`.\n1042\t- **Behavior:**\n1043\t  - They sway when idle, with an amplitude that follows the meter.\n1044\t  - They jump and cheer on big plays (by side), groan and sit on the other team's.\n1045\t  - They scramble where a home-run ball lands.\n1046\t  - **The wave** starts when the meter passes 70: a sine pulse running around the stands.\n1047\t- **Jumbotron** (a shape and a screen drawn by code): the batter's portrait and name; the replay; \"MAKE SOME NOISE!\",\n1048\t  \"BATZOOKA!\", \"SAFE?\"; the Derby leaders.\n1049\t- **Mascots:** a Blob mascot per home park dances on the dugout roof (squash on the beat, `audio.onBeat`) and stars in\n1050\t  the between-innings shows.\n1051\t- **Fireworks** (night parks) or **confetti cannons** (day parks) after home runs and wins.\n1052\t- **Between-innings shows**, one per half-inning (about 4 s, skippable with ✕):\n1053\t  - the **Mascot Race** (three mascots race the warning track; one always trips);\n1054\t  - **Dance Cam** (a fan dances on the jumbotron);\n1055\t  - the **T-Shirt Cannon** (shirts arc into the stands);\n1056\t  - the **Grounds Crew Shuffle** (rakers dance).\n1057\t- **Vendors:** babble shouts with text pops in the stands (\"PEANUTS!\", \"GET YER DOGS!\").\n1058\t\n1059\t**Tests:** T-CROWD-01..04 (reactions follow the events by side; the wave starts above 70; between-innings shows end and\n1060\thand back; the jumbotron shows the current batter).\n1061\t\n1062\t### 6.18 Game modes\n1063\t\n1064\t- **TOUR** (`33-tour.js`), the story (§7.5): Spring Training is offered first (skippable), then five road games in\n1065\t  order, each with its cutscenes, its rival and its hazard, ending with the MegaDome boss game.\n1066\t  - The **Tour Map** is a stylized county map with the bus moving along a dotted road between six pins, where you\n1067\t    choose Play next stop, Clubhouse, Save, or Title.\n1068\t  - A **loss** gives a \"RAIN CHECK\" card: Retry (same stop) or Clubhouse first. A win gives Fan Bucks, the rival's\n1069\t    reward item and the next pin.\n1070\t  - Tour games last 3 innings (setting \"Tour game length\": Standard 3 / Quick 1) at the chosen difficulty.\n1071\t- **EXHIBITION**: Team Select (§6.24), then any unlocked park, innings 1/3/5/9, difficulty, and 1–4 players. Ends at\n1072\t  the results screen, then Rematch / Change teams / Title.\n1073\t- **BATZOOKA DERBY** (`31-derby.js`): 1–4 players take turns (hot seat). Each round is 2:00 or 10 outs, whichever\n1074\t  comes first (setting). The pitcher is the Pitch-O-Matic 3000 machine.\n1075\t  - **Outs:** any swing that isn't a home run, and any taken strike. Taken balls are free.\n1076\t  - **Score:** each home run's distance in feet, plus target bonuses: the blimp +500, the MEGA-TRON +300, drones +150,\n1077\t    the batter's-eye bullseye +200. Every 5th pitch is a **Golden Ball** worth ×2.\n1078\t  - Three home runs in a row fill the meter, for one Batzooka swing.\n1079\t  - At the end, initials for the **Moonshot Leaders** table (`E.Arcade` table `'derby'` and its letter wheel).\n1080\t  - The AI Hub round uses these rules (§6.21).\n1081\t- **SPRING TRAINING** (`32-training.js`): eight lessons (§7.6) taught by Grandpa Ike, with goals, prompts in the\n1082\t  player's own glyphs, slow-motion assists, and a stamp when passed. Ends with the \"House Rules\" card. Each lesson can\n1083\t  be replayed.\n1084\t\n1085\t### 6.19 Progression, unlocks and saves\n1086\t\n1087\t- **Fan Bucks (FB):** a win is worth 500, a loss 150. Bonuses: +50 a home run, +100 a Batzooka, +200 a shutout, plus\n1088\t  park targets (silo +300, drones +150 each, MEGA-TRON +300).\n1089\t- **Clubhouse upgrades** (team-wide, three tiers each; §7.8): Lumber (power), Glue Gloves (glove), Jet Cleats (speed),\n1090\t  Cannon Arms (arm), Hype Machine (meter gain). Each rival beaten gives one free tier of one upgrade.\n1091\t- **Unlocks:** Exhibition starts with every team and park except the MegaMax Mechanicals and the MegaDome, which\n1092\t  unlock when you reach the Tour's final. Dev mode can unlock all.\n1093\t- **Saves:** `game.saves`, three slots and an autosave. The data:\n1094\t  `{ v: 1, tour: { stop, beaten: [], wins, losses }, fb, upgrades, rewards, unlocked, training: { done: [] }, stats, derbyBest }`.\n1095\t  - Autosave after each Tour game and lesson; manual save on the Tour Map (`saves.canSave` only there); the title's\n1096\t    CONTINUE loads `saves.latest()`.\n1097\t  - Settings save themselves (the engine). Derby tables live in `E.store` (the arcade module).\n1098\t  - With storage blocked, or in an AI Hub session, everything plays on for the visit.\n1099\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37baaf64-6acc-44ac-b38d-1fce3a552962","uuid":"be2e3843-7f85-4158-b7b5-4054ec86a533","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:00:28.336Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-060015__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","content":"4. The umpire's whistle: \"BREAK IT UP!\" Everyone dusts off and walks back.\n5. The side with more knockdowns gets +50 meter; a tie gives both +20. A comic \"ejection\" line follows, though nobody\n   is really ejected.\n6. The batter takes first base for the HBP.\n\n**Fighting:**\n- Stick: move. ✕ / K: punch combo (`E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook'])`). □ / J: shove (`'bash'`, `E.knockback` 160).\n  △ / I: grab and toss (within 12 U: the `throw` move; the foe flies 40 U). ○ / L: dodge (a dash).\n- Three hits knock a fighter down: pose `down`, dizzy stars for 2 s, then up again.\n- AI fighters approach the nearest foe and throw combos with a 0.25 s wind-up so they telegraph.\n- Fights between AI fighters with no human nearby are drawn as **fight clouds**: a smoke ball (`particles.smoke`) with\n  fists, feet and a cap popping out.\n\n**Tone:** cartoon slapstick only: bonks, slide whistles, \"POW\" stamps, no blood.\n\n**Tests:** T-RHU-01..06 (trigger conditions and the setting; duration; scoring by knockdowns; meter award; the batter\non first after; no stray state after it ends).\n\n### 6.10 Hazards, park features and wind (`18-hazards.js`)\n\nEach park has one hazard or feature, as a plug-in `{ init(M), update(M, dt), onBall(M, ball), draw(r), hud() }`. Its\nrate is × `TUNE.hazardRate`. It is always **telegraphed** (a sound, a warning stamp, smoke) and introduced by a radio\nline the first time it happens.\n\n| Park | Hazard / feature | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kernel Field | **Silo Shot** | A home run that hits the silo beyond right field pops it like popcorn: popcorn rains, +300 Fan Bucks, +20 meter. Corn stalks sway with the wind. |\n| Barnacle Bay Park | **Gull Patrol** and **Splash Zone** | Gulls circle above 200 U. A fly ball with its apex above 200 has a 25% chance per gull pass of being snatched; the gull carries it 60–140 U, then drops it from z 150 (still in play). A home run to right field lands in the bay: a splash and a boat horn. |\n| Caldera Field | **Lava Vents** | Two vents in the gaps (r 820, θ ±22°) erupt every 25 s (3 s of rumble and smoke first). A ball touching an erupting vent is launched (vz +600, a random push from `rng.play`); CPU fielders keep 30 U away and a human is knocked back. Ash falls; the outfield glows. |\n| Tranquility Dome | **Low Gravity** and **Meteor Showers** | Gravity ×0.55, exit speed ×0.8, jumps ×2, deep fences. Every 40 s a meteor shower leaves 3 craters (decals) in the outfield for the rest of the half-inning; a grounder rolling through one turns by ±40°. |\n| Boneyard Ballpark | **Phantom Fog** and **Ghost Gloves** | Fly balls above 120 U go invisible (only the shadow and the landing marker show) until they come back down. The Haunts' fielders reach +40 U in wall catches (they phase into the fence). |\n| MegaMax MegaDome | **Defense Drones**, the **MEGA-TRON** and the **Boss** | Three drones patrol the outfield sky (z 150–250) on `E.Path`s. A fly ball that hits one deflects; a Batzooka hit destroys it (an explosion, +150 Fan Bucks). A ball off the MEGA-TRON jumbotron in center field is worth +300. The boss rules are in §7.1. |\n\n**Wind** (the four open-air parks; the two domes have none): set per game from `rng.play`: a direction and 0–12 U/s² of push on\nballs above 60 U. A windsock (a shape) and the banners' sway show it, and a HUD arrow with mph. On the moon the radio jokes that there's no air.\n\n**Tests:** T-HAZ-01..08 (each hazard fires at its rate and has its effect on a scripted ball; telegraphs come before\nthe event; the hazard rate slider; wind changes range in the right direction).\n\n### 6.11 CPU AI and difficulty (`20-ai.js`)\n\nFour levels: **ROOKIE, PRO, ALL-STAR, BATZOOKA**.\n\n| | Rookie | Pro | All-Star | Batzooka |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CPU swing timing σ | 60 ms | 42 ms | 30 ms | 20 ms |\n| CPU aim error σ | 3.6 U | 2.6 U | 1.8 U | 1.2 U |\n| CPU discipline (takes a ball outside the zone) | 0.45 | 0.60 | 0.72 | 0.82 |\n| CPU release quality (mean) | 0.50 | 0.68 | 0.80 | 0.90 |\n| CPU aftertouch use | 0 | 0.3 | 0.6 | 0.9 |\n| CPU fielder reaction | 0.34 s | 0.25 s | 0.19 s | 0.14 s |\n| CPU pitch speed | ×0.85 | ×1.0 | ×1.07 | ×1.15 |\n| Human sweet spot and timing window | ×1.25 | ×1.0 | ×1.0 | ×0.9 |\n\nThe CPU skills are also × `TUNE.cpuBat`, `cpuPitch`, `cpuField` and `cpuRun`.\n\n- **CPU batter:** after its reaction delay it predicts the crossing point with an aim error and moves the reticle\n  there. It decides to swing from P(in zone) and its discipline, with a timing error from N(0, σ). It uses power swings\n  in hitter's counts (2-0, 3-1) and when it is strong; bunts now and then with a runner on first and no outs; and a\n  Batzooka when its meter is full.\n- **CPU pitcher:** a pitch-mix table by count (ahead: break it off; behind: heater) with the batter's power in mind. It\n  targets the corners, more as the level rises, and uses its super with two strikes when its meter is full.\n- **CPU fielders:** §6.5. **CPU runners:** advance by the time race with a margin from the level, tag up when they can\n  make it, steal by speed against the arm.\n- **The bot** (`BZ.bot`): runs these brains for player 1's side and turns their choices into **input actions**\n  (`input.hold`, `input.tap`, the reticle's target as stick moves). Tests, the scripted playthrough and the attract\n  mode use it to play the real game through the real controls.\n\n**Tests:** T-AI-01..08 (CPU vs CPU over seeded 9-inning games at Pro (6 games in the default run, 30 with `--full`;\nsims run with `M.fast = true`: no walk-ups, shows, replays or cinematics, and the CPU decides at once): runs per team per game between 3 and 9; K% of\nplate appearances 12–32%; HR per plate appearance 2–8%; batting average .230–.330; errors per game 0.2–1.5; no game\nover 40 minutes of game time; no stuck play, meaning every play ends within 20 s; harder levels win more against easier\nones).\n\n### 6.12 Cameras, transitions and replays (`21-cams.js`)\n\n**Plate Cam** (Mode 7): `m7.cam = { x: H.x + side, y: H.y + 72, h: 36, angle: −π/2, pitch: 9, fov: 46 }` (`TUNE.plateDist`,\n`plateHeight`, `plateFov`). `side` is 4 U toward the side away from the batter, so the zone is clear.\n- The Plate Cam setting has two choices: *Classic* (the default) and *Close* (dist 52, h 30, fov 42).\n- The sky is the park's `E.Backdrop`. The floor is the park TileMap.\n- Billboards each frame: pitcher, fielders, batter, catcher, base runners and on-deck hitter with `m7.rig`; light\n  towers and props with `m7.shape`; the fence, stand faces, crowd rows, foul poles and scoreboard with `m7.add` quads\n  and sprites (far to near); the ball and its shadow with `m7.add` (radius max(1.5, 1.6 · k) px, stitches, the trail of\n  a super).\n- The umpire isn't drawn in the Plate Cam (he'd fill the screen); his calls show as a cut-in portrait.\n- The catcher crouches below the zone, drawn at 85% opacity where he overlaps it.\n- Overlays: the zone (a 3×3 grid; the setting is Always / During the pitch / Off), the reticle or target marker, the\n  release ring, the crossing dot. These are drawn from `m7.project` of their world points.\n- On contact the camera shakes for 0.15 s.\n\n**Plate Cam → Broadcast Cam.** On fair contact or a pop-up: hit-stop 0.06 s (`game.freeze`), a white flash\n(`game.flash('#fff', .06)`), then a cut to the Broadcast Cam framing the plate. Within 0.3 s it takes up\n`cam.frame([ballShadow, ballDrawn, landingMarker, chaser], { margin: 48, zoom: [.5, 1.1] })` with `cam.smooth = .15`.\n- Fouls back over the screen stay in the Plate Cam (the ball shrinks up and away, then \"FOUL BALL\").\n- Pop-ups high over the infield go to the Broadcast Cam, zoomed in.\n\n**Broadcast Cam framing by play:**\n- the ball in the air: the ball, its marker and the chaser;\n- a fielder with the ball: him, the throw's target base and the lead runner;\n- runners only: the lead runner and the next base.\n\nZoom stays within 0.5–1.1 × `TUNE.fieldZoom`. A wide screen shows more of the park; an upright phone zooms in. After\nthe play, a cut back to the Plate Cam for the next pitch.\n\n**Moonshot Cam.** When the prediction says a fair ball will clear the fence by 10 U or more (or on any Batzooka hit):\n1. `game.timeScale = .4`; Mode 7 `m7.chase(ball, { back: 60, h: 20 })` looks along the flight over the fence;\n2. the landing in the stands, a target or the bay: crowd scramble, fireworks, confetti (Broadcast Cam at the landing\n   point);\n3. normal speed; the **trot** in the Broadcast Cam (the batter jogs the bases, high-fiving the coaches; ×2 speed;\n   skippable with ✕).\n\nThe whole sequence lasts about 4 s plus the trot.\n\n**Replays.** A ring buffer keeps the last 8 s of the world at 60 Hz: the ball, every body's x, y, z and facing, and each\nrig's update inputs. After a big play (home run, diving or wall catch, double play, bowl-over, Batzooka, robbed homer)\nthe replay runs at ×0.5 in the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45, a new angle on the play), with a \"REPLAY\" banner and a pulsing\ncorner, and the jumbotron shows it too. ✕ skips it. The \"Replays\" setting is On / Big plays only (default) / Off.\n\n**Cutscene cameras:** `game.tween` on a focus point and the zoom, run by the cutscene runner (§6.15).\n\n**Tests:** T-CAM-01..07 (the Plate Cam projects the zone fully on screen at 427×240 and 320×240 for both box sides;\nthe cut on contact; the framing keeps ball and chaser on screen across 200 seeded batted balls (from snapshots of their\nscreen positions); the Moonshot Cam trigger; the replay buffer replays positions exactly; skips work; timeScale is\nrestored).\n\n### 6.13 HUD, prompts and glyphs (`22-hud.js`, `01-input.js`)\n\n**Layout** for a TV at 10 feet, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H` with a 12-pixel safe margin (TV overscan), kept clear of\n`game.cornerArea()`:\n- **Scorebug**, top left (scale 1 text, the score at scale 2): AWAY and HOME abbreviations with runs; the inning with\n  ▲ or ▼; two out pips; the count as **B ●●○ S ●○**; a mini diamond with lit bases.\n- **Meters**, top right: the batting team's BATZOOKA gauge (a bat-shaped bar that fills with flame and blinks when\n  full); the turbo bar; the wind arrow with mph.\n- **Player card**, bottom left: a portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`), the name, one stat line (avg and HR this game) or the\n  pitcher's stamina bar.\n- **Prompts**, bottom center: 2–5 icon-and-verb pairs for the current context. An action not available now is greyed\n  with its reason (\"BATZOOKA 63%\").\n- **Radio box**: the announcers' lines with portraits, bottom left above the player card (2 lines, non-modal, auto\n  advance).\n- **Call stamps**, center top: STEE-RIKE!, BALL FOUR, FOUL BALL, SAFE!, YER OUT!, HOME RUN!, BATZOOKA!!!, DOUBLE PLAY!,\n  RHUBARB!. Drawn with `E.font.title`, scale 3–4, a 3-color gradient, a pop-in tween and shake.\n- **Pitch info** after each pitch: \"CURVE 71 MPH\" (supers show silly speeds: \"MEGA HEATER 131 MPH!!\").\n- **Radar** in the Broadcast Cam, top right under the meters: the diamond and outfield with fielders, runners and\n  the ball.\n- **Pitching select**: four pitch names around a diamond of face-button icons in their positions (△ top, ○ right,\n  ✕ bottom, □ left), plus \"R1 SUPER\"; the stamina bar. With the mouse: the wheel highlights a pitch, left click throws.\n- **Throw targets** in the Broadcast Cam: when a fielder holds the ball, each base shows a ring with its button's icon\n  (○ 1B, △ 2B, □ 3B, ✕ HOME). With the mouse the rings take hover (they grow) and clicks.\n\n**Rules:** the important information is at scale 2 or more (the 5×7 font at scale 1 is about 31 px tall on a 1080p\nTV); no screen shows more than about 7 items of text at once; information is never in color alone (shapes and icons\ntoo); every HUD piece hides during cutscenes and replays.\n\n**The glyph icon renderer** `BZ.glyph(g, label, x, y, size = 11)` draws the icon for the string `input.label(action)`\nreturns (the font cannot draw ✕ ○ □ △ in color):\n- PlayStation: a dark disc (#1b1d26) with a light rim and the symbol in its classic color: ✕ blue #7aa7ff,\n  ○ red #ff6b6b, □ pink #f08ad8, △ green #4fe0b0.\n- Xbox: a dark disc with a colored letter: A green #6cc04a, B red #e8584a, X blue #4a8ef0, Y yellow #f4c430.\n- Shoulders and triggers (L1, R1, L2, R2, LB, RB, LT, RT): rounded pills with the text.\n- Options, Create, Menu, View: small pills. L3, R3, LS, RS: a stick ring with L or R.\n- D-pad: a cross with the direction lit.\n- Keyboard: a keycap (a light rounded rectangle with dark text; wide for Space, Shift, Enter).\n- Mouse: a mouse outline with the left or right half filled, or the wheel lit.\n\n**The prompt builder** `BZ.prompts(ctx)` → `[[action, verb, enabled, why]]`, drawn as icon + verb.\n- Labels follow `game.input.lastDevice`: for a gamepad, the `glyphs` family (PlayStation by default, Xbox by setting);\n  for the keyboard, keys; for the mouse, the mouse buttons where the action has one and keys for the rest.\n- Each player's prompts use their own input (`p.input.label`).\n\n**Tests:** T-HUD-01..08 (`label` → icon type for every action on each device and family; the setting switches the\nfamily; prompts change with `lastDevice` within one step; nothing in the HUD overlaps the corner area or goes outside\nthe safe margins at 320×240, 427×240 and 320×690; greyed prompts give a reason).\n\n### 6.14 Input and controls (`01-input.js`)\n\n**One preset**, positional, passed as `new E.Game({ input: BZ.KEYS })` and on every scene. Actions are named by the\nbutton's position because the same button does a different job in each context (swing, fastball, throw home, punch).\nThat also keeps rebinding conflict-free. On the keyboard, the number keys and PC-standard keys come first (below), and\nthe I J K L cluster is an alternate set laid out like the face buttons.\n\n| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyph | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `south` | ✕ (Pad0) | A | Space, K | left button |\n| `east` | ○ (Pad1) | B | L | – |\n| `west` | □ (Pad2) | X | J | right button |\n| `north` | △ (Pad3) | Y | I | – |\n| `l1` | L1 (Pad4) | LB | Q, Tab | – |\n| `r1` | R1 (Pad5) | RB | E | – |\n| `l2` | L2 (Pad6) | LT | Left Shift | – |\n| `r2` | R2 (Pad7) | RT | C, Left Ctrl | – |\n| `view` | R3 (Pad11) | RS | V | – |\n| `up/down/left/right` | left stick, D-pad | | W A S D, arrows | – |\n| `confirm` | ✕ | A | Enter, Space, K | click a menu item |\n| `cancel` | ○ | B | Escape, Backspace, L | – |\n| `pause` / `start` | Options (Pad9) | Menu | Escape, P / Enter | – |\n| the reticle and pitch target | left stick | | W A S D, arrows | the pointer (absolute) |\n| `num1`–`num5` | (face buttons do these jobs) | | 1 2 3 4 5 | – |\n| pitch type | face buttons | | 1–5 (or K L J I, E) | wheel to pick, left button to throw |\n\n**What each button does, by context** (prompts and the Playbook show this per device):\n\n| Context | ✕ south | ○ east | □ west | △ north | L1 | R1 | L2 | R2 | stick or mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Batting | contact swing | – | power swing | bunt (hold) | steal: hold, plus a base button | arm the BATZOOKA | – | – | aim the reticle |\n| Pitching | heater | breaking ball | off-speed | signature | pickoff: hold, plus a base button | super pitch | – | – | aim; aftertouch in flight |\n| Fielding (holding the ball) | throw home | throw to 1B | throw to 3B | throw to 2B | switch fielder | Laser Arm (plus a base button) | turbo | – | run; click a base to throw |\n| Fielding (chasing) | – | – | – | – | switch fielder | jump / wall climb | turbo | dive | run |\n| Running | headfirst dive (near a base) | (base select) | (base select) | (base select) | all back (or one, with a base button) | all advance (or one, with a base button) | turbo; with R1, Afterburners | – | mouse: left-click a base to send, right-click to bring back |\n| Rhubarb | punch combo | dodge | shove | grab and toss | – | – | – | – | move |\n| Menus | select | back | – | – | previous tab | next tab | – | – | hover focuses, click selects |\n\n- **The keyboard is PC-native first:** WASD and the mouse aim and run; Space swings and confirms; the mouse's left\n  button swings, throws at a clicked base and selects; the right button is the power swing; **number keys pick**\n  (`num1`–`num5`: 1–5 the pitch type when pitching, 1–4 the base, first to home, wherever a base is chosen); Shift\n  is turbo (sprint); Tab switches fielder; E is the super and \"go\"; Q is the steal or \"back\" modifier; C or Ctrl\n  dives; Escape pauses. The I J K L cluster stays as an alternate set that mirrors the face buttons, for players who\n  like a pad layout on keys. Keyboard prompts show the number keys first.\n- **Base buttons:** a face button picks a base by its position (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home) in three cases:\n  when your fielder holds the ball (a throw), while L1 is held (a steal, a pickoff, sending one runner back), and while\n  R1 is held (sending one runner, or a Laser Arm throw). Otherwise the face buttons do their usual jobs.\n- **Mouse:** the pointer aims the bat or the pitch (pointer → zone plane by inverse-interpolating the zone's 4\n  projected corners). In the Broadcast Cam, `game.mouseGround()` within 26 U of a base hovers it. Fielding and running\n  use `input.suppressCode('Mouse0')` / `'Mouse2'` unless the click lands on a base, so a stray click never throws home.\n  The wheel is the game's in a match (`camera: { zoom: false }` on the match scene).\n- **Device tracking:** `lastDevice` switches on use; prompts follow it the next step. Moving the mouse counts as mouse\n  use.\n- **AI Hub rule:** in a session, Options is ignored while Create (Pad8) is held (the hub's bar).\n- **Local players 2–4** get their own `game.players[i]` inputs: a gamepad each, or half a keyboard each\n  (`E.Input.halfKeys`). The mouse belongs to player 1.\n- **Rumble:** contact (strength by Q), catches, collisions, Batzooka (strong), Rhubarb hits (`input.rumble`; vibration\n  setting).\n\n**Tests:** T-IN-01..10 (each context routes the right action; a press in a menu doesn't leak into play\n(`consumeAll`); the mouse mapping onto the zone; a base click throws and a stray click doesn't; the wheel and the number keys\npick pitch types, and the number keys pick bases; Create plus Options in a session is ignored; half-keyboard players; rebinding a positional action changes all\nof its contexts' prompts).\n\n### 6.15 Commentary, writing and babble voices (`23-talk.js`, `02-audio.js`)\n\n**The cast's voices:** HAP HARRIGAN (play-by-play; warm, quick, hyperbolic); \"BIG\" LOU BOMBECK (color; a retired\nslugger with malapropisms and tall tales); GLORIA VOX (the PA; slow, echoing); UMPIRE BARB HAMMERSMITH (calls with\ngusto); and in the Tour, the captains, GRANDPA IKE and MORTIMER MEGAMAX.\n\n**The commentary director:**\n- It listens to the bus and picks a line from the pool for the event's tag (Appendix C, §7.9).\n- Lines carry priority: 3 for a home run, Batzooka, Rhubarb, walk-off or robbery; 2 for outs, hits and steals; 1 for\n  routine plays. A higher priority line interrupts.\n- At most one line every 2.5 s. The same line isn't repeated within 20 lines.\n- Variables: `{batter} {pitcher} {team} {dist} {mph} {park} {score}`.\n- Hap and Lou alternate on big plays (Hap calls it, Lou reacts).\n- Lines show in the radio box (`E.Dialog` with `modal: false`, `auto: 2.4`, portrait, alpha .85). The PA and the\n  umpire show as stamps plus their own voice.\n- The \"Commentary\" setting is On / Big plays only / Off.\n\n**Writing style.** It is the source's style: baseball radio broadcasting, turned up.\n- Short punchy sentences in the present tense, and one image per line (\"That ball had a map and a flashlight\").\n- Baseball slang: dinger, tater, moonshot, frozen rope, can of corn, chin music, painting the black, Uncle Charlie, the\n  heater, cheese, seeing-eye single, worm-burner, Texas leaguer, ducks on the pond, the hot corner.\n- Running gags: Lou's stories about 1979; the vendor who always sells out; the mascot's bad luck.\n- Lines are no longer than about 90 characters.\n- Never mean, no profanity, no real players or teams, and no real quotes.\n\n**Babble** (Animalese/Simlish-style voices, `BZ.babble`):\n- **Hooks:** for dialog, set `talk.voice = null` and poll `talk.shown` each update; for each newly typed letter of\n  `talk.pages[talk.i]`, play a syllable. Stamps and the PA call `BZ.babble.say(text, voice)` straight.\n- **Syllables:** each letter a–z plays one note.\n  - Pitch = base × 2^((map[letter] + contour) / 12). Vowels are a 0, e +4, i +7, o +2, u −3, y +5; consonants a small\n    offset from a hash.\n  - Length = 1 / rate (× 1.3 for vowels, × 0.8 for consonants).\n  - Each note is `game.audio.sfx({ wave, freq, to: freq · 1.06, dur, vol, vib }, { vary: 0 })`. s, z and x add a\n    10-ms noise burst.\n- **Spaces and punctuation:** a space is a 40-ms gap; `.` `,` `!` `?` are longer pauses. After `!` the next 4 notes go\n  +3 semitones; `?` rises at the end. A word in capitals is louder and slower.\n- **Voice profiles:**\n\n| Voice | Wave | Base Hz | Rate (letters/s) | Other |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hap | pulse | 190 | 20 | range 7 |\n| Lou | square | 120 | 15 | vib [6, .02] (gravel) |\n| Gloria (PA) | triangle | 150 | 9 | an echo copy at +120 ms, 40% volume |\n| Umpire Barb | square | 260 | 14 | long vowels held with vibrato (\"STEE-RIIIKE\") |\n| Dot | pulse12 | 300 | 22 | |\n| Ike | triangle | 170 | 12 | vib [5, .04] (shaky) |\n| Mortimer | saw | 140 | 16 | nasal |\n| ENFORCER-9000 | saw plus square | 90 | 18 | pitch quantized to a 3-note arpeggio |\n| skeletons | triangle | 220 | 18 | noise \"clacks\" |\n| other captains | | | | a distinct base and wave each (§7.1) |\n\n- **Volume:** the \"Voices\" slider (`settings.define`, 0–1, default 0.8).\n- **Subtitles:** all speech is gibberish, so the text is always on screen.\n\n**Cutscene runner** `BZ.cut(steps)` runs a `game.script` over data steps:\n`['say', who, text]`, `['walk', who, x, y]`, `['pose', who, pose, expr]`, `['face', who, angle]`, `['cam', x, y, zoom, secs]`,\n`['sfx', name]`, `['music', song]`, `['fx', kind, x, y]`, `['wait', s]`, `['spawn', ...]`, `['call', fn]`.\n✕ advances a line; holding ○ for 1 s skips the scene (a ring fills around the icon).\n\n**Tests:** T-TALK-01..07 (every event tag has at least 6 lines; the rate limit; priority interrupts; variables fill in;\nno line over 90 characters; babble plays one syllable per letter, deterministically; the skip-hold works).\n\n### 6.16 Music and sound effects (`02-audio.js`, `56-songs.js`)\n\n**Music**, in the engine's song notation (`{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ name, wave, vol, notes, on }] }`). All original:\nno real anthems, and not even public-domain ballpark tunes. The ballpark **organ** is square + triangle an octave\napart with a slow vibrato.\n\n| Song | Mood and makeup |\n|---|---|\n| `title` \"Batzooka!\" | 140 bpm; organ riff + chip rock bass + drums; a rocket-whoosh sting on the logo |\n| `menu` \"Clubhouse Shuffle\" | laid-back organ shuffle, 100 bpm |\n| `tourmap` \"Barnstorm Boogie\" | road boogie, 120 bpm |\n| one in-game loop per park | Kernel Field county-fair organ; Barnacle Bay 6/8 shanty; Caldera minor drums and saw bass; Tranquility spacey arpeggios; Boneyard spooky organ with a theremin-like vibrato sine; MegaDome synthwave. Ducked at the plate; layers named `base`, `hype` (the meter full), `drums` (runners in scoring position) |\n| `rally` | an original organ \"rally\" climb, played when the home team puts runners on |\n| `derby` | breakneck surf rock, 160 bpm |\n| `rhubarb` | a frantic chase, 180 bpm |\n| `training` | light and bouncy |\n| `ending` and `credits` | an anthemic reprise of the title |\n| `hubcard` | a title variation, quieter |\n\n**Stings** (`audio.sting`, on the bar): home run fanfare, strikeout sting, Batzooka ignition, inning change,\nvictory, defeat, between-innings show.\n\n**Sound effects** (`audio.define`; vary by quality):\n- **Bat:** \"tink\" (weak), \"crack\" (solid), \"KRAKOOM\" (Batzooka: noise burst + low boom + ring).\n- **Gloves:** mitt \"POP\", glove \"thup\".\n- **Pitches:** a whoosh pitched by speed; foul tip; thud in the dirt.\n- **Ball:** bounce, fence clang or thud, pole \"ping\".\n- **Crowd:** a filtered-noise murmur loop as a layer, swelling with the meter; cheer; groan; \"OOOH\"; boos; gasp.\n- **Ballpark:** organ stabs, the PA's echo.\n- **Players:** footsteps, slide, dive thump, climb scrabble, throw whoosh, collision crash.\n- **Spectacle:** fireworks (boom + crackle), the Batzooka charge (a rising saw and noise), a rocket trail loop, the\n  engine's `explode`.\n- **Parks:** gull caw, lava rumble, meteor whoosh, ghost \"boo\", drone buzz, boat horn.\n- **Interface:** meter-full ding, turbo whoosh, menu select, confirm and cancel.\n- **Rhubarb:** bonk, slide whistle, \"pow\".\n\n**Tests:** T-AUD-01..04 (every sound name used is defined; every scene sets its music; no unknown-song or unknown-sound\nwarnings in `game.errors` or the console; the Voices setting scales babble).\n\n### 6.17 Crowd and ballpark life (`24-crowd.js`)\n\n- **Crowd seats** on the stand tiles: 2–3 people a tile, drawn as in the sports kit: little bodies in shirts of the home\n  and away colors, skin tones, caps. Over 1,500 in a park, drawn `r.queue` with `every: 2`.\n- **Behavior:**\n  - They sway when idle, with an amplitude that follows the meter.\n  - They jump and cheer on big plays (by side), groan and sit on the other team's.\n  - They scramble where a home-run ball lands.\n  - **The wave** starts when the meter passes 70: a sine pulse running around the stands.\n- **Jumbotron** (a shape and a screen drawn by code): the batter's portrait and name; the replay; \"MAKE SOME NOISE!\",\n  \"BATZOOKA!\", \"SAFE?\"; the Derby leaders.\n- **Mascots:** a Blob mascot per home park dances on the dugout roof (squash on the beat, `audio.onBeat`) and stars in\n  the between-innings shows.\n- **Fireworks** (night parks) or **confetti cannons** (day parks) after home runs and wins.\n- **Between-innings shows**, one per half-inning (about 4 s, skippable with ✕):\n  - the **Mascot Race** (three mascots race the warning track; one always trips);\n  - **Dance Cam** (a fan dances on the jumbotron);\n  - the **T-Shirt Cannon** (shirts arc into the stands);\n  - the **Grounds Crew Shuffle** (rakers dance).\n- **Vendors:** babble shouts with text pops in the stands (\"PEANUTS!\", \"GET YER DOGS!\").\n\n**Tests:** T-CROWD-01..04 (reactions follow the events by side; the wave starts above 70; between-innings shows end and\nhand back; the jumbotron shows the current batter).\n\n### 6.18 Game modes\n\n- **TOUR** (`33-tour.js`), the story (§7.5): Spring Training is offered first (skippable), then five road games in\n  order, each with its cutscenes, its rival and its hazard, ending with the MegaDome boss game.\n  - The **Tour Map** is a stylized county map with the bus moving along a dotted road between six pins, where you\n    choose Play next stop, Clubhouse, Save, or Title.\n  - A **loss** gives a \"RAIN CHECK\" card: Retry (same stop) or Clubhouse first. A win gives Fan Bucks, the rival's\n    reward item and the next pin.\n  - Tour games last 3 innings (setting \"Tour game length\": Standard 3 / Quick 1) at the chosen difficulty.\n- **EXHIBITION**: Team Select (§6.24), then any unlocked park, innings 1/3/5/9, difficulty, and 1–4 players. Ends at\n  the results screen, then Rematch / Change teams / Title.\n- **BATZOOKA DERBY** (`31-derby.js`): 1–4 players take turns (hot seat). Each round is 2:00 or 10 outs, whichever\n  comes first (setting). The pitcher is the Pitch-O-Matic 3000 machine.\n  - **Outs:** any swing that isn't a home run, and any taken strike. Taken balls are free.\n  - **Score:** each home run's distance in feet, plus target bonuses: the blimp +500, the MEGA-TRON +300, drones +150,\n    the batter's-eye bullseye +200. Every 5th pitch is a **Golden Ball** worth ×2.\n  - Three home runs in a row fill the meter, for one Batzooka swing.\n  - At the end, initials for the **Moonshot Leaders** table (`E.Arcade` table `'derby'` and its letter wheel).\n  - The AI Hub round uses these rules (§6.21).\n- **SPRING TRAINING** (`32-training.js`): eight lessons (§7.6) taught by Grandpa Ike, with goals, prompts in the\n  player's own glyphs, slow-motion assists, and a stamp when passed. Ends with the \"House Rules\" card. Each lesson can\n  be replayed.\n\n### 6.19 Progression, unlocks and saves\n\n- **Fan Bucks (FB):** a win is worth 500, a loss 150. Bonuses: +50 a home run, +100 a Batzooka, +200 a shutout, plus\n  park targets (silo +300, drones +150 each, MEGA-TRON +300).\n- **Clubhouse upgrades** (team-wide, three tiers each; §7.8): Lumber (power), Glue Gloves (glove), Jet Cleats (speed),\n  Cannon Arms (arm), Hype Machine (meter gain). Each rival beaten gives one free tier of one upgrade.\n- **Unlocks:** Exhibition starts with every team and park except the MegaMax Mechanicals and the MegaDome, which\n  unlock when you reach the Tour's final. Dev mode can unlock all.\n- **Saves:** `game.saves`, three slots and an autosave. The data:\n  `{ v: 1, tour: { stop, beaten: [], wins, losses }, fb, upgrades, rewards, unlocked, training: { done: [] }, stats, derbyBest }`.\n  - Autosave after each Tour game and lesson; manual save on the Tour Map (`saves.canSave` only there); the title's\n    CONTINUE loads `saves.latest()`.\n  - Settings save themselves (the engine). Derby tables live in `E.store` (the arcade module).\n  - With storage blocked, or in an AI Hub session, everything plays on for the visit.\n","numLines":400,"startLine":700,"totalLines":2144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01F6LfXWGdnH8v4LeUTaJ7wd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghvuBoFb6HNJvLgwGofi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XSLdbQkQ8xsePW71nJRu2p","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-060015__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","offset":1100,"limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13901,"cache_read_input_tokens":46402,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13901},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37baaf64-6acc-44ac-b38d-1fce3a552962","uuid":"cedb307a-10c5-46cf-9096-526e1a7a35f6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:00:30.421Z","request_id":"req_011CghvuBaMfu1weGvfF8uTG","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01XSLdbQkQ8xsePW71nJRu2p":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-060015__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","offset":1100,"limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XSLdbQkQ8xsePW71nJRu2p","type":"tool_result","content":"1100\t### 6.20 Local multiplayer (1–4 on one machine)\n1101\t\n1102\t- Joining happens on Team Select (and Derby Setup): `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`. Press Start on a free gamepad or 2 on\n1103\t  the keyboard (half a keyboard each). Joined players' cards appear in their `E.PLAYER_COLORS`.\n1104\t- **Sides:**\n1105\t  - Each player moves their card left (away) or right (home), and can drop out.\n1106\t  - A side's first human is its **Captain**, the second its **Co-captain**. Sides with no humans are CPU.\n1107\t  - So the game covers 1P vs CPU, 2P versus, 2P co-op vs CPU, 2v1, 2v2 and 3v1.\n1108\t- **Duties when a side has two humans:**\n1109\t  - Defense: the captain pitches, the co-captain controls the fielders after contact.\n1110\t  - Offense: the captain bats, the co-captain runs the runners (steals, orders).\n1111\t  - With one human, they do everything.\n1112\t- **The Derby** takes turns, one player at a time, each with their own device or a shared one.\n1113\t- **On screen:** one shared screen. The Plate Cam is shared; the Broadcast Cam frames the action. Each human's\n1114\t  controlled fielder or runner gets a marker arrow in their player color. Prompts show per player at their side of the\n1115\t  screen (P1 bottom left, P2 bottom right; P3 and P4 just above).\n1116\t- **Pausing:** anyone's Options pauses (\"P2 PAUSED\"); a lost controller pauses and names its player (the engine).\n1117\t\n1118\t**Tests:** T-MP-01..05 (four players join with `addPlayer`; side assignment; duty routing; each prompt uses its own\n1119\tplayer's device; a removed player's side falls back to the CPU).\n1120\t\n1121\t### 6.21 AI Hub integration: the online Derby (`40-hub.js`)\n1122\t\n1123\t**The mode is `ghost`, 1–4 players.** Everyone plays the same Derby round on their own device, from the same pitch\n1124\tsequence, and sees the others as ghosts. The others' home runs fly through your sky as comets in their seat color, and\n1125\ttheir batters stand as translucent holograms in the on-deck circles and on the dugout rails, swinging when they swing.\n1126\tGhosts never affect your round.\n1127\t\n1128\tWhy not a shared-world head-to-head game: real batting timing does not survive 150 ms of latency and 50 ms of jitter\n1129\t(the hub's own test conditions), and a host-authoritative baseball world is a lot of risk for a demo. A head-to-head\n1130\tgame stays local (§6.20).\n1131\t\n1132\t**Page tags**, in `<head>`, after the client:\n1133\t```html\n1134\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n1135\t<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n1136\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n1137\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n1138\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n1139\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n1140\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Hit the most home-run feet in two minutes\">\n1141\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim: stick/WASD/mouse · Swing: ✕/Space/click · Power: □/J/right-click\">\n1142\t```\n1143\tThe client `vendor/aihub-session.js` is pasted unmodified as the **first script in `<head>`, right after\n1144\t`<meta charset>`** (§12).\n1145\t\n1146\t**Flow when `AIHub.active`:**\n1147\t1. Boot skips the title, menus and attract mode. The boot steps run, then the `hubDerby` scene in its `loading` state.\n1148\t2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. With `null`, the game goes on\n1149\t   normally to the title.\n1150\t3. Build the round: the MegaDome at night; the pitch sequence from `BZ.rng.deal = AIHub.rng('pitches')`; wind and\n1151\t   targets from `AIHub.rng('park')`. Then call **`AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press**.\n1152\t4. **Our own card** (`hubCard`; its look is the game's):\n1153\t   - the logo and \"BATZOOKA DERBY · ONLINE\";\n1154\t   - the players lined up as batters in their seat colors (`#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`) with a READY\n1155\t     check;\n1156\t   - \"How to win: hit the most home-run feet in 2:00\";\n1157\t   - this player's controls as icons for their device;\n1158\t   - the time left from the `card` event (or \"Starting…\" when `starting`), \"Waiting for more time…\" when `paused`, and\n1159\t     \"You asked for more time\" when `asked`.\n1160\t   \n1161\t   ✕, Enter or a click on READY sends `AIHub.answer('ready')`; △, M or a click on MORE TIME sends\n1162\t   `AIHub.answer('more')`. The card **never calls `Math.random`**: no particles or weather, and sounds with `vary: 0`.\n1163\t   It has no menus and changes nothing.\n1164\t5. `start`: a 3-2-1 countdown while `AIHub.time() < 0`. Play begins at `AIHub.time() >= 0`, and the round clock is\n1165\t   `min(120000, info.limitMs − 5000) − AIHub.time()` (just 120000 when `limitMs` is missing or 0).\n1166\t6. **Reporting:**\n1167\t   - Every second: `AIHub.progress({ score, label: '1,284 ft · 4 HR · 3 outs' })`, from 0 onward. (The harness only\n1168\t     presses arrows and WASD, so progress must flow without hits.)\n1169\t   - When the round ends (clock or 10 outs): `AIHub.result({ score, stats: { 'Home runs': n, 'Longest': '512 ft', Outs: k }, awards: [{ title: 'Moonshot', detail: '512 ft' }] })`,\n1170\t     **once**.\n1171\t   - On `time-up`: report at once if not yet reported.\n1172\t7. **Ghost traffic**, at most 20 messages a second and well under 16 KB:\n1173\t   - 10 Hz unreliable `{ t: 'g', area: 'derby', s: swingPhase, rx, rz, sc: score, o: outs }`;\n1174\t   - a reliable `{ t: 'hit', n, p: [x, y, z], v: [vx, vy, vz], spin, kind, dist, bz }` on each batted ball, which\n1175\t     receivers simulate locally with the same physics (cosmetic);\n1176\t   - a reliable `{ t: 'done', score }` at the end.\n1177\t   \n1178\t   `leave` removes that ghost; `message` from unknown ids is ignored.\n1179\t8. **`end`:** stop input (an `ended` flag: the scene ignores input and the game sends nothing), stop the sound\n1180\t   (`audio.music(null)` and the effects muted), and show \"ROUND OVER\" with the standings drawn on the canvas. Names\n1181\t   are canvas text, never HTML.\n1182\t\n1183\t**Session rules:**\n1184\t- **No title or restart:** Quit to title, Restart, Dev Mode and Pitching Change are hidden, and the back gesture is\n1185\t  swallowed.\n1186\t- **No popups or sockets:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, new windows or sockets.\n1187\t- **Storage:** the client already gives an empty storage, so add nothing for it.\n1188\t- **Pause:** the pause menu offers only Resume, Settings and Controls, and the round clock keeps running.\n1189\t\n1190\t**Tests:**\n1191\t- Headless T-HUB-01..08 use a mock `AIHub` in the vm context: connect, ready without input, the card's answers, start\n1192\t  at time 0, progress every second, a single result, time-up, end stopping input and sound, ghost messages at ≤ 20/s\n1193\t  and applied without changing the local score, and `Math.random` not called during the card.\n1194\t- Browser: the real harness in Playwright (§11.7).\n1195\t\n1196\t### 6.22 Attract mode (`35-attract.js`)\n1197\t\n1198\t- **Starts** after **10 s with no input** on the title (its mode menu included) or the other top-level menus (the Tour\n1199\t  menu, Team Select before anyone joins, Derby Setup, the lesson list, About). Input means any key, button, click,\n1200\t  touch, stick past the dead zone, or mouse movement over 4 px.\n1201\t- **Exits** on any input: back to the title (input consumed, so it doesn't pick a menu item); the idle timer restarts.\n1202\t- **What it is:** a looping **highlight reel played by the real game**, run by a director over `game.debug` with\n1203\t  cheats on (forced pitches and contact, infinite meter, no outs).\n1204\t- **On screen:** a \"DEMO PLAY\" banner and a pulsing \"PRESS ANY BUTTON\" (the icon for the device used last; on\n1205\t  touch-only devices \"TOUCH TO RETURN\"), plus a caption card between segments.\n1206\t- **Segments** (§7.11) cover the Plate Cam duel, super pitches, fielding, a diving catch, a Laser Arm throw, a steal and\n1207\t  slide, a bowl-over, a Batzooka moonshot with fireworks, a Rhubarb, low-gravity wall climbs, the Phantom Ball, the\n1208\t  drones and boss, the Derby with ghost comets, and the Moonshot Leaders table. One loop takes about 2.5 minutes.\n1209\t- **Robustness:** each segment has a time limit and moves on by itself if its event doesn't happen.\n1210\t- **Never** in an AI Hub session.\n1211\t\n1212\t**Tests:** T-ATT-01..05 (idle 10 s on the title starts it; it doesn't start during play or with the dev panel open;\n1213\tevery segment runs in order without errors and the reel loops; any input returns to the title; touch-only mode still\n1214\truns it).\n1215\t\n1216\t### 6.23 Dev mode (`36-dev.js`)\n1217\t\n1218\t**Reached** from the pause menu's **Dev Mode** item (any pausable scene; hidden in hub sessions). It is a large,\n1219\t10-foot-readable panel over the frozen game, with six tabs. L1/R1 or Q/E change tabs; up and down move; left and right\n1220\tchange a value (hold to accelerate); ✕ toggles or activates; △ resets this value (or pins a slider); ○ closes. The\n1221\tmouse hovers, clicks, and drags sliders.\n1222\t\n1223\t1. **TUNE**: every `BZ.TUNE` value as a **live slider** grouped as in Appendix A (Pitching, Batting, Ball, Players,\n1224\t   Over the top, CPU, Camera, Game). Values apply instantly.\n1225\t   - **Reset to defaults** for all, and a reset per group.\n1226\t   - \"Keep tuning between visits\" stores the values in `E.store('bz:tune')`.\n1227\t   - **Pinned sliders:** △ pins up to 3. While playing, a thin strip at the top shows them; hold Create (Pad8) with\n1228\t     the D-pad to adjust (keyboard: `[` and `]` adjust, `\\` picks the next pin).\n1229\t2. **SITUATION**: set the inning, half, outs, balls, strikes and score; runners on first, second and third; the\n1230\t   batter; the pitcher's stamina; both meters and turbo; the park; the wind. Then Apply.\n1231\t3. **CHEATS** (toggles):\n1232\t   - Batting and pitching: perfect contact always; every pitch a strike.\n1233\t   - Meters: infinite meter, infinite turbo.\n1234\t   - Play: fielders ignore walls (no clip); the CPU plays my side (autoplay); slow motion ×0.25; freeze the AI.\n1235\t   - Show: the zone always; the predicted pitch path and the timing bar; landing markers and AI targets; catch\n1236\t     radii and hitboxes.\n1237\t4. **TRIGGERS**:\n1238\t   - Force the next pitch (type and spot); force a home run on the next contact.\n1239\t   - Hazard now; meteor shower; Rhubarb; Batzooka cinematic; replay the last play.\n1240\t   - End the half-inning; walk-off now; win the game; lose the game.\n1241\t5. **WARP**:\n1242\t   - Scenes: title, attract, the gallery, the hub card (mocked), credits.\n1243\t   - Play: any Tour stop (pregame, game or postgame), any lesson, the Derby, an Exhibition in any park.\n1244\t   - Save: unlock all, +5,000 FB, reset the save.\n1245\t6. **INFO**:\n1246\t   - Live: fps, scene and phase, the ball's position and velocity, the last contact's numbers (timing ms, offset, Q,\n1247\t     EV, launch, spray), the seed, the input device and glyph family, the last 8 events, `E.versionLabel()`.\n1248\t   - **Frame step** (one 1/120 s step while frozen) and **Copy snapshot** (JSON to the clipboard).\n1249\t\n1250\t**The animation gallery** (from WARP) shows every new animation on rigs in turn, at normal speed or slowed, for review\n1251\twith `tools/filmstrip.mjs`.\n1252\t\n1253\t**Tests:** T-DEV-01..06 (the pause menu holds Dev Mode; a slider changes `TUNE` and the effect shows in the next pitch;\n1254\treset restores the defaults exactly; the situation applies; cheats toggle; warps land in their scene with no errors).\n1255\t\n1256\t### 6.24 Screens and UX flow (`34-menus.js`)\n1257\t\n1258\t**Principles:**\n1259\t- Big cards, never tables: at most 7 items to a screen.\n1260\t- The focus is obvious: it scales up 10%, gets a bright border and bobs gently.\n1261\t- A legend at the bottom shows glyph icons (\"✕ Select · ○ Back\").\n1262\t- Every screen works with the D-pad/stick, the keyboard (arrows/WASD, Enter/K, Escape/L) and the mouse (hover moves\n1263\t  the focus, click selects). Menus are pixel art drawn with `E.ui.box`, `E.font` and `E.font.title`.\n1264\t\n1265\t**Title:**\n1266\t- The background is the Kernel Field at dusk, seen from the Broadcast Cam, slowly panning, with fireflies; Dot\n1267\t  Dynamite stands with the Batzooka on her shoulder.\n1268\t- The logo \"BATZOOKA BASEBALL\" is drawn with `E.font.title` (scale 4, a gold-orange gradient, depth 3) and a rocket\n1269\t  trail sweeping under it.\n1270\t- Menu: CONTINUE (when there is a save), TOUR, EXHIBITION, DERBY, SPRING TRAINING, OPTIONS, ABOUT.\n1271\t- `E.versionLabel()` in a corner.\n1272\t\n1273\t**Team Select:**\n1274\t- Two big team cards (left away, right home): the captain's rig turning, the team name, three stat stars (Power,\n1275\t  Speed, Pitching), and the park they play in.\n1276\t- Left and right change the team; up and down move between the team, park, innings and difficulty rows.\n1277\t- The join row shows P1–P4 cards that slide to a side.\n1278\t\n1279\t**Results:**\n1280\t- The final score big; a one-line line score (R H E).\n1281\t- The game's **three stars** as cards (portrait, one line each).\n1282\t- FB earned, with a counting-up tween.\n1283\t- Buttons: Continue, Replay highlights, Rematch.\n1284\t\n1285\t**Playbook** (help, from the title's OPTIONS and the pause menu): one page per context (Batting, Pitching, Fielding,\n1286\tRunning, Supers, Rhubarb, House Rules). Each shows a picture of the action in the game's own art (a rig in the pose)\n1287\tand 3–5 big icon + verb lines for the current device.\n1288\t\n1289\t**About/Credits:** §6.27.\n1290\t\n1291\t**Tests:** T-UI-01..06 (every screen reachable and back; mouse hover moves the focus; clicks select; keyboard and pad\n1292\tnavigation; no screen over 7 items; the legend icons match the device).\n1293\t\n1294\t### 6.25 Settings and accessibility\n1295\t\n1296\tOurs are added with `menu: { settings: [...] }` / `settings.define`, beside the engine's own (volume, music, effects,\n1297\treduce motion, shake, flashes, pixel scaling, screen filter, frame rate, controls, dead zone, vibration):\n1298\t- **Gameplay:** Difficulty (Rookie / Pro / All-Star / Batzooka); Exhibition innings (1/3/5/9); Tour game length\n1299\t  (Standard / Quick); Mercy rule (On/Off); Comeback Boost (On/Off); Rhubarbs (On/Off); Derby length (2:00 / 10 outs).\n1300\t- **Assists:** Batting assist (Off / Timing hints / Big sweet spot); Pitching assist (Off / Auto-aim); Fielding\n1301\t  (Manual / Assisted / Auto); Baserunning (Manual / Auto).\n1302\t- **Display:** Timing feedback (On/Off); Strike zone (Always / During pitch / Off); Plate Cam (Classic / Close);\n1303\t  Field camera (Broadcast / High / Isometric); Replays (On / Big plays / Off).\n1304\t- **Sound:** Commentary (On / Big plays / Off); Voices (a 0–1 slider).\n1305\t- **Controller:** Button symbols (PlayStation / Xbox), the engine's setting with our default (§3.3).\n1306\t- **Developer:** \"Keep dev tuning\" (On/Off).\n1307\t\n1308\tReduce motion also softens the camera cuts (fades instead of flashes) and turns off the screen shake in cinematics.\n1309\t\n1310\t### 6.26 Touch-only devices\n1311\t\n1312\t- **Detection, at boot:**\n1313\t  `touchOnly = matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`.\n1314\t  It clears for good when a key is pressed or a gamepad connects (`gamepadconnected`, or `input.padId` set).\n1315\t- **While touch-only:**\n1316\t  - The title shows a large card: a controller and a keyboard icon, \"Controller or keyboard required\", and a big\n1317\t    **Settings** button (it opens `game.menu('settings')`), beside the engine's corner menu button.\n1318\t  - There are no touch controls: `game.input.touchEnabled = false`, no scene has a `touch` list, and the engine's\n1319\t    touch-button settings stay hidden.\n1320\t  - The attract mode runs after 10 s as usual; a tap returns to the title card.\n1321\t  - Tapping menu items does nothing (the menus need a controller or keyboard), except the Settings button and the\n1322\t    corner button.\n1323\t\n1324\t**Tests:** T-TOUCH-01..03 (`#touch` forces the flag: the title shows the card, the menus ignore taps but Settings opens;\n1325\tthe attract mode starts after 10 s; a key press clears the flag).\n1326\t\n1327\t### 6.27 About/Credits, Source Information and the exit interview\n1328\t\n1329\t**The About/Credits screen** (from the title's ABOUT) has pages turned by\n1330\tL1/R1, left/right or clicks. Each page holds at most about 12 lines at scale 1, wrapped to the width.\n1331\t1. **ABOUT:** the logo; \"An original over-the-top arcade baseball game\"; the version (`E.versionLabel()`); the controls\n1332\t   in one line.\n1333\t2. **SOURCE INFORMATION**, every field TASK.md lists, filled in at the end of the build (\"unknown\" for anything that\n1334\t   can't be confirmed):\n1335\t   - Date: 2026-11-03 17:48:19.\n1336\t   - Source: baseball (the sport), in the over-the-top arcade style.\n1337\t   - Plan: `PLAN.md`.\n1338\t   - Genre and Perspective: §2.\n1339\t   - Tech: stack, engine and module versions, the changes made, mocap, the AI Hub client version, and Node and\n1340\t     Playwright for the tests.\n1341\t   - Art Direction, Animation, Audio: as built.\n1342\t   - Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, a ghost-mode Derby round for 1–4 players, client 1.4.0.\n1343\t   - Testing: what was allowed and used.\n1344\t   - Research: what was read.\n1345\t   - Environment: \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\", or what is known.\n1346\t   - Model: as TASK.md gives it.\n1347\t   - Usage: estimates, or \"unknown\".\n1348\t3. **TESTING FEEDBACK:** the four answers.\n1349\t4. **ENGINE FEEDBACK:** the six answers (including what changed in the engine, §3.3).\n1350\t5. **PROMPT FEEDBACK:** the four answers.\n1351\t6. **CREDITS:**\n1352\t   - Design, code, art, music and writing: the model that built the game (named as in Source Information).\n1353\t   - Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (MIT, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge).\n1354\t   - Motion: the CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses); Quaternius and Mesh2Motion (CC0).\n1355\t   - AI Hub Session client 1.4.0 (michaelcrosato/ai-hub).\n1356\t   - Any CC0 asset sets used (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG).\n1357\t   - \"Thanks for playing!\"\n1358\t\n1359\t**The same text** goes in the builder's final chat reply, and in `exit-interview.json` in exactly TASK.md's shape (§12).\n1360\tIts `aiHub` block should read:\n1361\t- `name`: \"Batzooka Baseball\"; `tagline`: \"Swing for the moon. Literally.\"\n1362\t- `description`: \"Over-the-top arcade baseball: fireball pitches, a rocket-powered bat, wall-climbing catches and\n1363\t  bench-clearing rhubarbs on a barnstorming tour to save a cornfield ballpark.\"\n1364\t- `type`: \"Game\"; `genre`: \"Sports\"; `source`: \"Baseball (over-the-top arcade style)\"; `series`: \"\"; `players`: \"1–4\".\n1365\t- `controls`: \"Stick/WASD/mouse aim · ✕/Space/click swing · □/J/right-click power · R1/E Batzooka\".\n1366\t- `tags`: [\"arcade\", \"comedy\", \"versus\", \"pixel art\", \"boss fights\", \"retro\"].\n1367\t\n1368\t---\n1369\t\n1370\t## 7. Content\n1371\t\n1372\tAll names, teams, parks and lines are original. Stats run 1–10. Batters have POW (power), CON (contact), SPD (speed),\n1373\tARM and GLV (glove); pitchers have VEL (velocity), CTL (control), BRK (break) and STA (stamina). Stat 5 is the ×1.0\n1374\tbaseline: a stat's factor is `1 + 0.03 · (stat − 5)` (0.88–1.15), unless a formula says otherwise. B/T is the side\n1375\tthey bat / throw from.\n1376\t\n1377\t### 7.1 Teams and rosters (`50-teams.js`)\n1378\t\n1379\t**1. Kernel Falls COMETS** (COM). The heroes: a scrappy farm-town team. Home: Kernel Field.\n1380\t- **Uniform:** cream jerseys and pants `#f4ecd8`, navy trim, cap and belt `#1e2a5a`, gold socks `#f2c230`.\n1381\t- **Mascot:** Popcorn Pete, a cream Blob with butter-yellow spots and feet.\n1382\t- **Team super pitch:** **Corn Popper**: the ball pops three times in flight like a kernel, jittering ±3 U at each pop\n1383\t  with a \"pok!\" sound.\n1384\t- **Lineup:**\n1385\t\n1386\t| Pos | Name | B/T | POW CON SPD ARM GLV (pitchers: VEL CTL BRK STA) | Look and notes |\n1387\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1388\t| CF | **Dot \"Dynamite\" Delgado** (captain) | R/R | 9 8 7 7 7 | heroic build, ponytail, warm brown skin; wields the Batzooka; voice pulse12 300 Hz |\n1389\t| SS | Pep Porter | L/R | 4 8 9 7 9 | twin; chibi build, freckles |\n1390\t| 2B | Pip Porter | R/R | 4 7 9 6 8 | Pep's twin, mirror palette |\n1391\t| 1B | Moose Magnusson | L/L | 9 5 3 5 6 | bulky, blond braid |\n1392\t| 3B | Sunny Sato | R/R | 6 7 6 8 8 | always smiling (`expr: 'smile'`) |\n1393\t| C | Rosa \"Radar\" Reyes | R/R | 6 6 4 9 8 | mask, catcher's mitt |\n1394\t| LF | Lefty Luna | L/L | 5 8 8 6 7 | slap hitter |\n1395\t| RF | Hank \"Hambone\" Hollis | R/R | 8 5 5 9 6 | bulky; eats between pitches |\n1396\t| P | **Grandpa Ike Kowalski** | R/R | 4 8 9 6 | hunch .3, bald, white sides; signature **Butterfly Knuckler**; voice triangle 170 Hz, shaky; the coach |\n1397\t| RP | Kid Kernel | R/R | 9 5 5 4 | teenager; all heat |\n1398\t\n1399\t**2. Harbor City KRAKENS** (HCK). Home: Barnacle Bay Park.\n1400\t- **Uniform:** teal `#1f8a8a`, coral trim `#ff7a5c`, sand pants `#e8d8b0`, sailor-stripe socks.\n1401\t- **Mascot:** Squidge, a purple Blob with a big mouth.\n1402\t- **Captain and ace:** **Captain Bettina Brine** (P, R/L, 7 7 8 7). Signature **Riptide Curve** (a curve that dips,\n1403\t  rises and dips: a sine of amplitude 4 U along its path). Team super **Tidal Wave** (the ball rides a translucent wave\n1404\t  sprite that rises 10 U and crashes down through the zone). Voice: pulse at 200 Hz.\n1405\t- **Lineup:** C \"Barnacle\" Barney Finn · 1B Coral Quinn · 2B Skip Marlin · SS Wade Waters · 3B Gil Gruber ·\n1406\t  LF Shelly Shoals · CF Dash Seaborne · RF Hook Hardy · RP Rip Tidewell.\n1407\t\n1408\t**3. Magma City ERUPTORS** (MCE). Home: Caldera Field.\n1409\t- **Uniform:** charcoal `#2b2b30`, lava orange `#ff6a1a` with yellow trim; caps with flame bills.\n1410\t- **Mascot:** Sparky, a glowing orange Blob that drips embers.\n1411\t- **Captain and ace:** **Iggy \"The Igniter\" Blaze** (P, R/R, 9 6 7 7). Signature **Magma Sinker** (a late drop of\n1412\t  −10 U with an ember trail). Team super **Meteor Heater** (a fireball at 1.35× speed with a flame trail and heat\n1413\t  shimmer; the catcher's mitt smokes). Voice: square at 230 Hz, fast. He is a hothead, and he starts the Tour's\n1414\t  scripted Rhubarb.\n1415\t- **Lineup:** C Basalt Bruno · 1B Magnus Holt · 2B Cinder Ellis · SS Ashley Pyre · 3B \"Hot Corner\" Hector Flores ·\n1416\t  LF Ember Okafor · CF Flint Rocha · RF Sol Vulcan · RP Scorch McGee.\n1417\t\n1418\t**4. Moonbase METEORS** (MBM). Home: Tranquility Dome.\n1419\t- **Uniform:** silver `#c8d0e0`, cosmic purple `#6a4cff`, white; `hat: 'visor'` space helmets in the field.\n1420\t- **Mascot:** Moonpie, a grey cratered Blob that floats.\n1421\t- **Captain and ace:** **Commander Cosmo Vega** (P, L/L, 6 8 9 8). Signature **Zero-G Floater** (slow, 0.62×; it\n1422\t  rises 6 U and then drops 12 U late). Team super **Black Hole Ball** (the ball shrinks to a dot with a dark swirl,\n1423\t  and a batter's reticle within 10 U is pulled 3 U toward it). Voice: sine at 210 Hz with vibrato.\n1424\t- **Lineup:** C Orbit Okoye · 1B Nova Nakamura · 2B Stella Petrov · SS Quasar Diaz · 3B Rocket Rhodes ·\n1425\t  LF Astra Bell · CF Halley Brooks · RF Gus Gravitas · RP Pulsar Penn.\n1426\t\n1427\t**5. Hollowmoor HAUNTS** (HMH). Home: Boneyard Ballpark. Players are `build: 'skeleton'` with `colors.bone`.\n1428\t- **Uniform:** bone `#e8e0c8`, midnight `#1a1630`, ghost green `#7dffb0`; tattered caps.\n1429\t- **Mascot:** Boo, a white Blob with a front face, drawn translucent.\n1430\t- **Captain and ace:** **Count Rattlebones** (P, R/R, 7 8 9 9). Signature **Phantom Forkball** (invisible for\n1431\t  u 0.35–0.8, then a sharp −8 U drop). Team super **Banshee Ball** (invisible until u 0.85, with a scream sound and\n1432\t  a screen flicker). Voice: triangle at 220 Hz with clacks.\n1433\t- **Lineup:** C Mort Graves · 1B Boney Malone · 2B Wailin' Winnie · SS Skelly Kelly · 3B Ezekiel Tomb · LF Shade Murphy ·\n1434\t  CF Wisp O'Hara · RF Digger Doyle · RP Spook Hennessy.\n1435\t\n1436\t**6. MegaMax MECHANICALS** (MMX). The boss team. Home: the MegaMax MegaDome. Players are robot Humanoids: metal\n1437\tcolors, `eyeGlow`, helmets, and `build: 'bulky'` for some.\n1438\t- **Uniform:** chrome `#b8c0cc`, corporate red `#e01e3c`, neon cyan `#2de2e6`.\n1439\t- **Mascot:** Mr. Synergy, a chrome Blob with wings: a hovering drone with a smiley screen.\n1440\t- **Ace and boss:** **ENFORCER-9000** (P, R/R, 10 8 6 10, `size: 2.2`, bulky, red eye glow). Signature **Gigavolt\n1441\t  Heater** (1.25× with an electric zig-zag of ±2 U). Team super **Overclock Laser** (1.5×, perfectly straight, with a\n1442\t  laser trail; only hittable with a GOOD timing or better).\n1443\t- **Boss rules:**\n1444\t  - **Overclock:** his pitch speed rises 5% each inning.\n1445\t  - **Short circuit:** a home run off him short-circuits him for the next half-inning: speed ×0.85, sparks, a\n1446\t    stutter in his windup.\n1447\t  - **Rally Cap:** if the Comets trail entering their last turn at bat, the stands (full of the beaten rival teams)\n1448\t    start the Rally Cap chant and the Comets' meter fills.\n1449\t- **Lineup:** C Backstop-X · 1B Forklift · 2B Servo · SS Sprocket · 3B Torque · LF Router · CF Byte · RF Piston ·\n1450\t  RP Spare Part.\n1451\t\n1452\t**Non-players:**\n1453\t- **Mortimer Megamax:** the villain, owner of MegaMax Corp. `outfit: 'coat'`, `hat: 'tophat'` (§3.3), a gold watch\n1454\t  chain; voice saw at 140 Hz. He watches from a luxury box in the MegaDome.\n1455\t- **HAP HARRIGAN:** headset band, sweater vest.\n1456\t- **BIG LOU BOMBECK:** bulky, bald, a 1979 championship ring.\n1457\t- **GLORIA VOX:** the PA, a portrait only.\n1458\t- **UMPIRE BARB HAMMERSMITH:** navy coat, cap and mask.\n1459\t- **Grounds crew, vendors, fans:** generic rigs and the crowd.\n1460\t\n1461\t### 7.2 Parks (`51-parks.js`)\n1462\t\n1463\t| Park | Team | Time and sky | Fence LF / LC / CF / RC / RF (U), height | Ground beyond | Lights | Weather | Music |\n1464\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1465\t| **Kernel Field** | Comets | golden late afternoon: a custom Backdrop with hills, farmland, a windmill, clouds, a low sun | 980 / 1080 / 1180 / 1060 / 950, h 40 | cornfield (rows of `cornstalk` shapes), the silo in right, a red-barn scoreboard | ambient 0.85, warm sun tint; string lights come on in the evening | light leaves | county-fair organ |\n1466\t| **Barnacle Bay Park** | Krakens | bright noon: the `ocean` preset plus gulls | 1000 / 1100 / 1150 / 1000 / 900 (RF h 30), others h 40 | the bay beyond right (a `water` floor with bobbing boats), a pier, a lighthouse in left | lights off (day) | wind out to left | 6/8 shanty |\n1467\t| **Caldera Field** | Eruptors | dusk: a dark red sky, the volcano, a smoke plume | 920 / 1060 / 1200 / 1100 / 1000, h 50 (basalt) | a lava moat (a `lava` floor, glowing `[r, g, b, glow]`), basalt columns | ambient 0.55; orange lava lights along the fence; 4 light towers | ash | minor drums, saw bass |\n1468\t| **Tranquility Dome** | Meteors | lunar night: the `space` preset, a big Earth disc, the dome's ribs drawn as arcs | 1350 / 1450 / 1550 / 1450 / 1350, h 60 | regolith with craters, habitat modules, antennas, a rover | ambient 0.55; cold blue-white floods | none (\"no air, no wind, no excuses\") | spacey arpeggios |\n1469\t| **Boneyard Ballpark** | Haunts | midnight: a full moon and fog layers | 1000 / 1080 / 1120 / 1080 / 1000, h 36 (an iron cemetery fence) | tombstones, crypts, dead trees, lanterns, candles | ambient 0.45; green and blue lanterns; jack-o'-lantern glow | `leaves` weather, plus fog as Backdrop `fog` layers | spooky organ, theremin |\n1470\t| **MegaMax MegaDome** | Mechanicals | neon night: the `city-night` preset seen through the dome's glass ribs, holo-billboards | 1000 / 1060 / 1100 / 1060 / 1000, h 40; the **Mega Wall** in left (θ −45° to −20°) is h 90 | a neon concourse floor (cyan grid lines), the luxury box, drones | ambient 0.6; magenta and cyan neon lights; the MEGA-TRON's glow | none (indoors) | synthwave |\n1471\t\n1472\t**Per-park physics** (in the park def): `gravityMul` (moon 0.55), `evMul` (moon 0.8), `jumpMul` (moon 2.0), `bounceMul`\n1473\t(dome turf 1.1), and the outfielders' depth shift (moon +200 U). **Floor materials:** grass with the park's mow pattern\n1474\t(Kernel stripes, Barnacle checkerboard, Caldera dark burnt grass, Tranquility bright astroturf, Boneyard dead brown-green,\n1475\tMegaDome turf with cyan neon lines).\n1476\t\n1477\t### 7.3 Pitch catalog (`11-pitch.js` data)\n1478\t\n1479\tThe heater's base speed is 340 U/s. Break is in U at the plate (+x is toward the glove side for a right-handed\n1480\tpitcher); k is the lateness exponent.\n1481\t\n1482\t| Pitch | Speed × | Break x, z | k | Special |\n1483\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1484\t| Heater (fastball) | 1.00 | 0, +1.5 | 1.0 | a little \"rise\" |\n1485\t| Curve (\"Uncle Charlie\") | 0.74 | +7, −10 | 2.2 | |\n1486\t| Slider | 0.86 | +9, −3 | 2.0 | |\n1487\t| Changeup (\"the Pillow\") | 0.66 | −3, −6 | 1.6 | |\n1488\t| Sinker | 0.92 | −4, −6 | 1.8 | |\n1489\t| Knuckler | 0.60 | 0, −3 | – | wobble: noise of amplitude 4 at 3 Hz from the pitch's own seed |\n1490\t| *Signatures* | | | | Butterfly Knuckler (knuckler ×1.6 wobble, it flutters), Riptide Curve, Magma Sinker, Zero-G Floater, Phantom Forkball, Gigavolt Heater (§7.1) |\n1491\t| *Team supers* | ×1.35 unless stated | | | Corn Popper, Tidal Wave, Meteor Heater, Black Hole Ball, Banshee Ball, Overclock Laser (§7.1) |\n1492\t| Pitch-O-Matic (Derby) | 0.80 | small random breaks from `rng.deal` | – | 80% in the zone; every 5th is a Golden Ball |\n1493\t\n1494\tRepertoires: Ike (heater, curve, knuckler, Butterfly); Kid Kernel (heater, slider, changeup, signature = a 1.15×\n1495\t\"Kernel Cannon\" heater); each rival ace (heater, curve or slider, changeup or sinker, signature); relievers (heater,\n1496\tslider, changeup, the team's signature).\n1497\t\n1498\t### 7.4 Supers\n1499\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37baaf64-6acc-44ac-b38d-1fce3a552962","uuid":"e1e7b401-2399-4146-9743-9b001fb255ec","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:00:30.582Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-060015__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","content":"### 6.20 Local multiplayer (1–4 on one machine)\n\n- Joining happens on Team Select (and Derby Setup): `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`. Press Start on a free gamepad or 2 on\n  the keyboard (half a keyboard each). Joined players' cards appear in their `E.PLAYER_COLORS`.\n- **Sides:**\n  - Each player moves their card left (away) or right (home), and can drop out.\n  - A side's first human is its **Captain**, the second its **Co-captain**. Sides with no humans are CPU.\n  - So the game covers 1P vs CPU, 2P versus, 2P co-op vs CPU, 2v1, 2v2 and 3v1.\n- **Duties when a side has two humans:**\n  - Defense: the captain pitches, the co-captain controls the fielders after contact.\n  - Offense: the captain bats, the co-captain runs the runners (steals, orders).\n  - With one human, they do everything.\n- **The Derby** takes turns, one player at a time, each with their own device or a shared one.\n- **On screen:** one shared screen. The Plate Cam is shared; the Broadcast Cam frames the action. Each human's\n  controlled fielder or runner gets a marker arrow in their player color. Prompts show per player at their side of the\n  screen (P1 bottom left, P2 bottom right; P3 and P4 just above).\n- **Pausing:** anyone's Options pauses (\"P2 PAUSED\"); a lost controller pauses and names its player (the engine).\n\n**Tests:** T-MP-01..05 (four players join with `addPlayer`; side assignment; duty routing; each prompt uses its own\nplayer's device; a removed player's side falls back to the CPU).\n\n### 6.21 AI Hub integration: the online Derby (`40-hub.js`)\n\n**The mode is `ghost`, 1–4 players.** Everyone plays the same Derby round on their own device, from the same pitch\nsequence, and sees the others as ghosts. The others' home runs fly through your sky as comets in their seat color, and\ntheir batters stand as translucent holograms in the on-deck circles and on the dugout rails, swinging when they swing.\nGhosts never affect your round.\n\nWhy not a shared-world head-to-head game: real batting timing does not survive 150 ms of latency and 50 ms of jitter\n(the hub's own test conditions), and a host-authoritative baseball world is a lot of risk for a demo. A head-to-head\ngame stays local (§6.20).\n\n**Page tags**, in `<head>`, after the client:\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Hit the most home-run feet in two minutes\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim: stick/WASD/mouse · Swing: ✕/Space/click · Power: □/J/right-click\">\n```\nThe client `vendor/aihub-session.js` is pasted unmodified as the **first script in `<head>`, right after\n`<meta charset>`** (§12).\n\n**Flow when `AIHub.active`:**\n1. Boot skips the title, menus and attract mode. The boot steps run, then the `hubDerby` scene in its `loading` state.\n2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. With `null`, the game goes on\n   normally to the title.\n3. Build the round: the MegaDome at night; the pitch sequence from `BZ.rng.deal = AIHub.rng('pitches')`; wind and\n   targets from `AIHub.rng('park')`. Then call **`AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press**.\n4. **Our own card** (`hubCard`; its look is the game's):\n   - the logo and \"BATZOOKA DERBY · ONLINE\";\n   - the players lined up as batters in their seat colors (`#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`) with a READY\n     check;\n   - \"How to win: hit the most home-run feet in 2:00\";\n   - this player's controls as icons for their device;\n   - the time left from the `card` event (or \"Starting…\" when `starting`), \"Waiting for more time…\" when `paused`, and\n     \"You asked for more time\" when `asked`.\n   \n   ✕, Enter or a click on READY sends `AIHub.answer('ready')`; △, M or a click on MORE TIME sends\n   `AIHub.answer('more')`. The card **never calls `Math.random`**: no particles or weather, and sounds with `vary: 0`.\n   It has no menus and changes nothing.\n5. `start`: a 3-2-1 countdown while `AIHub.time() < 0`. Play begins at `AIHub.time() >= 0`, and the round clock is\n   `min(120000, info.limitMs − 5000) − AIHub.time()` (just 120000 when `limitMs` is missing or 0).\n6. **Reporting:**\n   - Every second: `AIHub.progress({ score, label: '1,284 ft · 4 HR · 3 outs' })`, from 0 onward. (The harness only\n     presses arrows and WASD, so progress must flow without hits.)\n   - When the round ends (clock or 10 outs): `AIHub.result({ score, stats: { 'Home runs': n, 'Longest': '512 ft', Outs: k }, awards: [{ title: 'Moonshot', detail: '512 ft' }] })`,\n     **once**.\n   - On `time-up`: report at once if not yet reported.\n7. **Ghost traffic**, at most 20 messages a second and well under 16 KB:\n   - 10 Hz unreliable `{ t: 'g', area: 'derby', s: swingPhase, rx, rz, sc: score, o: outs }`;\n   - a reliable `{ t: 'hit', n, p: [x, y, z], v: [vx, vy, vz], spin, kind, dist, bz }` on each batted ball, which\n     receivers simulate locally with the same physics (cosmetic);\n   - a reliable `{ t: 'done', score }` at the end.\n   \n   `leave` removes that ghost; `message` from unknown ids is ignored.\n8. **`end`:** stop input (an `ended` flag: the scene ignores input and the game sends nothing), stop the sound\n   (`audio.music(null)` and the effects muted), and show \"ROUND OVER\" with the standings drawn on the canvas. Names\n   are canvas text, never HTML.\n\n**Session rules:**\n- **No title or restart:** Quit to title, Restart, Dev Mode and Pitching Change are hidden, and the back gesture is\n  swallowed.\n- **No popups or sockets:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, new windows or sockets.\n- **Storage:** the client already gives an empty storage, so add nothing for it.\n- **Pause:** the pause menu offers only Resume, Settings and Controls, and the round clock keeps running.\n\n**Tests:**\n- Headless T-HUB-01..08 use a mock `AIHub` in the vm context: connect, ready without input, the card's answers, start\n  at time 0, progress every second, a single result, time-up, end stopping input and sound, ghost messages at ≤ 20/s\n  and applied without changing the local score, and `Math.random` not called during the card.\n- Browser: the real harness in Playwright (§11.7).\n\n### 6.22 Attract mode (`35-attract.js`)\n\n- **Starts** after **10 s with no input** on the title (its mode menu included) or the other top-level menus (the Tour\n  menu, Team Select before anyone joins, Derby Setup, the lesson list, About). Input means any key, button, click,\n  touch, stick past the dead zone, or mouse movement over 4 px.\n- **Exits** on any input: back to the title (input consumed, so it doesn't pick a menu item); the idle timer restarts.\n- **What it is:** a looping **highlight reel played by the real game**, run by a director over `game.debug` with\n  cheats on (forced pitches and contact, infinite meter, no outs).\n- **On screen:** a \"DEMO PLAY\" banner and a pulsing \"PRESS ANY BUTTON\" (the icon for the device used last; on\n  touch-only devices \"TOUCH TO RETURN\"), plus a caption card between segments.\n- **Segments** (§7.11) cover the Plate Cam duel, super pitches, fielding, a diving catch, a Laser Arm throw, a steal and\n  slide, a bowl-over, a Batzooka moonshot with fireworks, a Rhubarb, low-gravity wall climbs, the Phantom Ball, the\n  drones and boss, the Derby with ghost comets, and the Moonshot Leaders table. One loop takes about 2.5 minutes.\n- **Robustness:** each segment has a time limit and moves on by itself if its event doesn't happen.\n- **Never** in an AI Hub session.\n\n**Tests:** T-ATT-01..05 (idle 10 s on the title starts it; it doesn't start during play or with the dev panel open;\nevery segment runs in order without errors and the reel loops; any input returns to the title; touch-only mode still\nruns it).\n\n### 6.23 Dev mode (`36-dev.js`)\n\n**Reached** from the pause menu's **Dev Mode** item (any pausable scene; hidden in hub sessions). It is a large,\n10-foot-readable panel over the frozen game, with six tabs. L1/R1 or Q/E change tabs; up and down move; left and right\nchange a value (hold to accelerate); ✕ toggles or activates; △ resets this value (or pins a slider); ○ closes. The\nmouse hovers, clicks, and drags sliders.\n\n1. **TUNE**: every `BZ.TUNE` value as a **live slider** grouped as in Appendix A (Pitching, Batting, Ball, Players,\n   Over the top, CPU, Camera, Game). Values apply instantly.\n   - **Reset to defaults** for all, and a reset per group.\n   - \"Keep tuning between visits\" stores the values in `E.store('bz:tune')`.\n   - **Pinned sliders:** △ pins up to 3. While playing, a thin strip at the top shows them; hold Create (Pad8) with\n     the D-pad to adjust (keyboard: `[` and `]` adjust, `\\` picks the next pin).\n2. **SITUATION**: set the inning, half, outs, balls, strikes and score; runners on first, second and third; the\n   batter; the pitcher's stamina; both meters and turbo; the park; the wind. Then Apply.\n3. **CHEATS** (toggles):\n   - Batting and pitching: perfect contact always; every pitch a strike.\n   - Meters: infinite meter, infinite turbo.\n   - Play: fielders ignore walls (no clip); the CPU plays my side (autoplay); slow motion ×0.25; freeze the AI.\n   - Show: the zone always; the predicted pitch path and the timing bar; landing markers and AI targets; catch\n     radii and hitboxes.\n4. **TRIGGERS**:\n   - Force the next pitch (type and spot); force a home run on the next contact.\n   - Hazard now; meteor shower; Rhubarb; Batzooka cinematic; replay the last play.\n   - End the half-inning; walk-off now; win the game; lose the game.\n5. **WARP**:\n   - Scenes: title, attract, the gallery, the hub card (mocked), credits.\n   - Play: any Tour stop (pregame, game or postgame), any lesson, the Derby, an Exhibition in any park.\n   - Save: unlock all, +5,000 FB, reset the save.\n6. **INFO**:\n   - Live: fps, scene and phase, the ball's position and velocity, the last contact's numbers (timing ms, offset, Q,\n     EV, launch, spray), the seed, the input device and glyph family, the last 8 events, `E.versionLabel()`.\n   - **Frame step** (one 1/120 s step while frozen) and **Copy snapshot** (JSON to the clipboard).\n\n**The animation gallery** (from WARP) shows every new animation on rigs in turn, at normal speed or slowed, for review\nwith `tools/filmstrip.mjs`.\n\n**Tests:** T-DEV-01..06 (the pause menu holds Dev Mode; a slider changes `TUNE` and the effect shows in the next pitch;\nreset restores the defaults exactly; the situation applies; cheats toggle; warps land in their scene with no errors).\n\n### 6.24 Screens and UX flow (`34-menus.js`)\n\n**Principles:**\n- Big cards, never tables: at most 7 items to a screen.\n- The focus is obvious: it scales up 10%, gets a bright border and bobs gently.\n- A legend at the bottom shows glyph icons (\"✕ Select · ○ Back\").\n- Every screen works with the D-pad/stick, the keyboard (arrows/WASD, Enter/K, Escape/L) and the mouse (hover moves\n  the focus, click selects). Menus are pixel art drawn with `E.ui.box`, `E.font` and `E.font.title`.\n\n**Title:**\n- The background is the Kernel Field at dusk, seen from the Broadcast Cam, slowly panning, with fireflies; Dot\n  Dynamite stands with the Batzooka on her shoulder.\n- The logo \"BATZOOKA BASEBALL\" is drawn with `E.font.title` (scale 4, a gold-orange gradient, depth 3) and a rocket\n  trail sweeping under it.\n- Menu: CONTINUE (when there is a save), TOUR, EXHIBITION, DERBY, SPRING TRAINING, OPTIONS, ABOUT.\n- `E.versionLabel()` in a corner.\n\n**Team Select:**\n- Two big team cards (left away, right home): the captain's rig turning, the team name, three stat stars (Power,\n  Speed, Pitching), and the park they play in.\n- Left and right change the team; up and down move between the team, park, innings and difficulty rows.\n- The join row shows P1–P4 cards that slide to a side.\n\n**Results:**\n- The final score big; a one-line line score (R H E).\n- The game's **three stars** as cards (portrait, one line each).\n- FB earned, with a counting-up tween.\n- Buttons: Continue, Replay highlights, Rematch.\n\n**Playbook** (help, from the title's OPTIONS and the pause menu): one page per context (Batting, Pitching, Fielding,\nRunning, Supers, Rhubarb, House Rules). Each shows a picture of the action in the game's own art (a rig in the pose)\nand 3–5 big icon + verb lines for the current device.\n\n**About/Credits:** §6.27.\n\n**Tests:** T-UI-01..06 (every screen reachable and back; mouse hover moves the focus; clicks select; keyboard and pad\nnavigation; no screen over 7 items; the legend icons match the device).\n\n### 6.25 Settings and accessibility\n\nOurs are added with `menu: { settings: [...] }` / `settings.define`, beside the engine's own (volume, music, effects,\nreduce motion, shake, flashes, pixel scaling, screen filter, frame rate, controls, dead zone, vibration):\n- **Gameplay:** Difficulty (Rookie / Pro / All-Star / Batzooka); Exhibition innings (1/3/5/9); Tour game length\n  (Standard / Quick); Mercy rule (On/Off); Comeback Boost (On/Off); Rhubarbs (On/Off); Derby length (2:00 / 10 outs).\n- **Assists:** Batting assist (Off / Timing hints / Big sweet spot); Pitching assist (Off / Auto-aim); Fielding\n  (Manual / Assisted / Auto); Baserunning (Manual / Auto).\n- **Display:** Timing feedback (On/Off); Strike zone (Always / During pitch / Off); Plate Cam (Classic / Close);\n  Field camera (Broadcast / High / Isometric); Replays (On / Big plays / Off).\n- **Sound:** Commentary (On / Big plays / Off); Voices (a 0–1 slider).\n- **Controller:** Button symbols (PlayStation / Xbox), the engine's setting with our default (§3.3).\n- **Developer:** \"Keep dev tuning\" (On/Off).\n\nReduce motion also softens the camera cuts (fades instead of flashes) and turns off the screen shake in cinematics.\n\n### 6.26 Touch-only devices\n\n- **Detection, at boot:**\n  `touchOnly = matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`.\n  It clears for good when a key is pressed or a gamepad connects (`gamepadconnected`, or `input.padId` set).\n- **While touch-only:**\n  - The title shows a large card: a controller and a keyboard icon, \"Controller or keyboard required\", and a big\n    **Settings** button (it opens `game.menu('settings')`), beside the engine's corner menu button.\n  - There are no touch controls: `game.input.touchEnabled = false`, no scene has a `touch` list, and the engine's\n    touch-button settings stay hidden.\n  - The attract mode runs after 10 s as usual; a tap returns to the title card.\n  - Tapping menu items does nothing (the menus need a controller or keyboard), except the Settings button and the\n    corner button.\n\n**Tests:** T-TOUCH-01..03 (`#touch` forces the flag: the title shows the card, the menus ignore taps but Settings opens;\nthe attract mode starts after 10 s; a key press clears the flag).\n\n### 6.27 About/Credits, Source Information and the exit interview\n\n**The About/Credits screen** (from the title's ABOUT) has pages turned by\nL1/R1, left/right or clicks. Each page holds at most about 12 lines at scale 1, wrapped to the width.\n1. **ABOUT:** the logo; \"An original over-the-top arcade baseball game\"; the version (`E.versionLabel()`); the controls\n   in one line.\n2. **SOURCE INFORMATION**, every field TASK.md lists, filled in at the end of the build (\"unknown\" for anything that\n   can't be confirmed):\n   - Date: 2026-11-03 17:48:19.\n   - Source: baseball (the sport), in the over-the-top arcade style.\n   - Plan: `PLAN.md`.\n   - Genre and Perspective: §2.\n   - Tech: stack, engine and module versions, the changes made, mocap, the AI Hub client version, and Node and\n     Playwright for the tests.\n   - Art Direction, Animation, Audio: as built.\n   - Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, a ghost-mode Derby round for 1–4 players, client 1.4.0.\n   - Testing: what was allowed and used.\n   - Research: what was read.\n   - Environment: \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\", or what is known.\n   - Model: as TASK.md gives it.\n   - Usage: estimates, or \"unknown\".\n3. **TESTING FEEDBACK:** the four answers.\n4. **ENGINE FEEDBACK:** the six answers (including what changed in the engine, §3.3).\n5. **PROMPT FEEDBACK:** the four answers.\n6. **CREDITS:**\n   - Design, code, art, music and writing: the model that built the game (named as in Source Information).\n   - Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (MIT, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge).\n   - Motion: the CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses); Quaternius and Mesh2Motion (CC0).\n   - AI Hub Session client 1.4.0 (michaelcrosato/ai-hub).\n   - Any CC0 asset sets used (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG).\n   - \"Thanks for playing!\"\n\n**The same text** goes in the builder's final chat reply, and in `exit-interview.json` in exactly TASK.md's shape (§12).\nIts `aiHub` block should read:\n- `name`: \"Batzooka Baseball\"; `tagline`: \"Swing for the moon. Literally.\"\n- `description`: \"Over-the-top arcade baseball: fireball pitches, a rocket-powered bat, wall-climbing catches and\n  bench-clearing rhubarbs on a barnstorming tour to save a cornfield ballpark.\"\n- `type`: \"Game\"; `genre`: \"Sports\"; `source`: \"Baseball (over-the-top arcade style)\"; `series`: \"\"; `players`: \"1–4\".\n- `controls`: \"Stick/WASD/mouse aim · ✕/Space/click swing · □/J/right-click power · R1/E Batzooka\".\n- `tags`: [\"arcade\", \"comedy\", \"versus\", \"pixel art\", \"boss fights\", \"retro\"].\n\n---\n\n## 7. Content\n\nAll names, teams, parks and lines are original. Stats run 1–10. Batters have POW (power), CON (contact), SPD (speed),\nARM and GLV (glove); pitchers have VEL (velocity), CTL (control), BRK (break) and STA (stamina). Stat 5 is the ×1.0\nbaseline: a stat's factor is `1 + 0.03 · (stat − 5)` (0.88–1.15), unless a formula says otherwise. B/T is the side\nthey bat / throw from.\n\n### 7.1 Teams and rosters (`50-teams.js`)\n\n**1. Kernel Falls COMETS** (COM). The heroes: a scrappy farm-town team. Home: Kernel Field.\n- **Uniform:** cream jerseys and pants `#f4ecd8`, navy trim, cap and belt `#1e2a5a`, gold socks `#f2c230`.\n- **Mascot:** Popcorn Pete, a cream Blob with butter-yellow spots and feet.\n- **Team super pitch:** **Corn Popper**: the ball pops three times in flight like a kernel, jittering ±3 U at each pop\n  with a \"pok!\" sound.\n- **Lineup:**\n\n| Pos | Name | B/T | POW CON SPD ARM GLV (pitchers: VEL CTL BRK STA) | Look and notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CF | **Dot \"Dynamite\" Delgado** (captain) | R/R | 9 8 7 7 7 | heroic build, ponytail, warm brown skin; wields the Batzooka; voice pulse12 300 Hz |\n| SS | Pep Porter | L/R | 4 8 9 7 9 | twin; chibi build, freckles |\n| 2B | Pip Porter | R/R | 4 7 9 6 8 | Pep's twin, mirror palette |\n| 1B | Moose Magnusson | L/L | 9 5 3 5 6 | bulky, blond braid |\n| 3B | Sunny Sato | R/R | 6 7 6 8 8 | always smiling (`expr: 'smile'`) |\n| C | Rosa \"Radar\" Reyes | R/R | 6 6 4 9 8 | mask, catcher's mitt |\n| LF | Lefty Luna | L/L | 5 8 8 6 7 | slap hitter |\n| RF | Hank \"Hambone\" Hollis | R/R | 8 5 5 9 6 | bulky; eats between pitches |\n| P | **Grandpa Ike Kowalski** | R/R | 4 8 9 6 | hunch .3, bald, white sides; signature **Butterfly Knuckler**; voice triangle 170 Hz, shaky; the coach |\n| RP | Kid Kernel | R/R | 9 5 5 4 | teenager; all heat |\n\n**2. Harbor City KRAKENS** (HCK). Home: Barnacle Bay Park.\n- **Uniform:** teal `#1f8a8a`, coral trim `#ff7a5c`, sand pants `#e8d8b0`, sailor-stripe socks.\n- **Mascot:** Squidge, a purple Blob with a big mouth.\n- **Captain and ace:** **Captain Bettina Brine** (P, R/L, 7 7 8 7). Signature **Riptide Curve** (a curve that dips,\n  rises and dips: a sine of amplitude 4 U along its path). Team super **Tidal Wave** (the ball rides a translucent wave\n  sprite that rises 10 U and crashes down through the zone). Voice: pulse at 200 Hz.\n- **Lineup:** C \"Barnacle\" Barney Finn · 1B Coral Quinn · 2B Skip Marlin · SS Wade Waters · 3B Gil Gruber ·\n  LF Shelly Shoals · CF Dash Seaborne · RF Hook Hardy · RP Rip Tidewell.\n\n**3. Magma City ERUPTORS** (MCE). Home: Caldera Field.\n- **Uniform:** charcoal `#2b2b30`, lava orange `#ff6a1a` with yellow trim; caps with flame bills.\n- **Mascot:** Sparky, a glowing orange Blob that drips embers.\n- **Captain and ace:** **Iggy \"The Igniter\" Blaze** (P, R/R, 9 6 7 7). Signature **Magma Sinker** (a late drop of\n  −10 U with an ember trail). Team super **Meteor Heater** (a fireball at 1.35× speed with a flame trail and heat\n  shimmer; the catcher's mitt smokes). Voice: square at 230 Hz, fast. He is a hothead, and he starts the Tour's\n  scripted Rhubarb.\n- **Lineup:** C Basalt Bruno · 1B Magnus Holt · 2B Cinder Ellis · SS Ashley Pyre · 3B \"Hot Corner\" Hector Flores ·\n  LF Ember Okafor · CF Flint Rocha · RF Sol Vulcan · RP Scorch McGee.\n\n**4. Moonbase METEORS** (MBM). Home: Tranquility Dome.\n- **Uniform:** silver `#c8d0e0`, cosmic purple `#6a4cff`, white; `hat: 'visor'` space helmets in the field.\n- **Mascot:** Moonpie, a grey cratered Blob that floats.\n- **Captain and ace:** **Commander Cosmo Vega** (P, L/L, 6 8 9 8). Signature **Zero-G Floater** (slow, 0.62×; it\n  rises 6 U and then drops 12 U late). Team super **Black Hole Ball** (the ball shrinks to a dot with a dark swirl,\n  and a batter's reticle within 10 U is pulled 3 U toward it). Voice: sine at 210 Hz with vibrato.\n- **Lineup:** C Orbit Okoye · 1B Nova Nakamura · 2B Stella Petrov · SS Quasar Diaz · 3B Rocket Rhodes ·\n  LF Astra Bell · CF Halley Brooks · RF Gus Gravitas · RP Pulsar Penn.\n\n**5. Hollowmoor HAUNTS** (HMH). Home: Boneyard Ballpark. Players are `build: 'skeleton'` with `colors.bone`.\n- **Uniform:** bone `#e8e0c8`, midnight `#1a1630`, ghost green `#7dffb0`; tattered caps.\n- **Mascot:** Boo, a white Blob with a front face, drawn translucent.\n- **Captain and ace:** **Count Rattlebones** (P, R/R, 7 8 9 9). Signature **Phantom Forkball** (invisible for\n  u 0.35–0.8, then a sharp −8 U drop). Team super **Banshee Ball** (invisible until u 0.85, with a scream sound and\n  a screen flicker). Voice: triangle at 220 Hz with clacks.\n- **Lineup:** C Mort Graves · 1B Boney Malone · 2B Wailin' Winnie · SS Skelly Kelly · 3B Ezekiel Tomb · LF Shade Murphy ·\n  CF Wisp O'Hara · RF Digger Doyle · RP Spook Hennessy.\n\n**6. MegaMax MECHANICALS** (MMX). The boss team. Home: the MegaMax MegaDome. Players are robot Humanoids: metal\ncolors, `eyeGlow`, helmets, and `build: 'bulky'` for some.\n- **Uniform:** chrome `#b8c0cc`, corporate red `#e01e3c`, neon cyan `#2de2e6`.\n- **Mascot:** Mr. Synergy, a chrome Blob with wings: a hovering drone with a smiley screen.\n- **Ace and boss:** **ENFORCER-9000** (P, R/R, 10 8 6 10, `size: 2.2`, bulky, red eye glow). Signature **Gigavolt\n  Heater** (1.25× with an electric zig-zag of ±2 U). Team super **Overclock Laser** (1.5×, perfectly straight, with a\n  laser trail; only hittable with a GOOD timing or better).\n- **Boss rules:**\n  - **Overclock:** his pitch speed rises 5% each inning.\n  - **Short circuit:** a home run off him short-circuits him for the next half-inning: speed ×0.85, sparks, a\n    stutter in his windup.\n  - **Rally Cap:** if the Comets trail entering their last turn at bat, the stands (full of the beaten rival teams)\n    start the Rally Cap chant and the Comets' meter fills.\n- **Lineup:** C Backstop-X · 1B Forklift · 2B Servo · SS Sprocket · 3B Torque · LF Router · CF Byte · RF Piston ·\n  RP Spare Part.\n\n**Non-players:**\n- **Mortimer Megamax:** the villain, owner of MegaMax Corp. `outfit: 'coat'`, `hat: 'tophat'` (§3.3), a gold watch\n  chain; voice saw at 140 Hz. He watches from a luxury box in the MegaDome.\n- **HAP HARRIGAN:** headset band, sweater vest.\n- **BIG LOU BOMBECK:** bulky, bald, a 1979 championship ring.\n- **GLORIA VOX:** the PA, a portrait only.\n- **UMPIRE BARB HAMMERSMITH:** navy coat, cap and mask.\n- **Grounds crew, vendors, fans:** generic rigs and the crowd.\n\n### 7.2 Parks (`51-parks.js`)\n\n| Park | Team | Time and sky | Fence LF / LC / CF / RC / RF (U), height | Ground beyond | Lights | Weather | Music |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Kernel Field** | Comets | golden late afternoon: a custom Backdrop with hills, farmland, a windmill, clouds, a low sun | 980 / 1080 / 1180 / 1060 / 950, h 40 | cornfield (rows of `cornstalk` shapes), the silo in right, a red-barn scoreboard | ambient 0.85, warm sun tint; string lights come on in the evening | light leaves | county-fair organ |\n| **Barnacle Bay Park** | Krakens | bright noon: the `ocean` preset plus gulls | 1000 / 1100 / 1150 / 1000 / 900 (RF h 30), others h 40 | the bay beyond right (a `water` floor with bobbing boats), a pier, a lighthouse in left | lights off (day) | wind out to left | 6/8 shanty |\n| **Caldera Field** | Eruptors | dusk: a dark red sky, the volcano, a smoke plume | 920 / 1060 / 1200 / 1100 / 1000, h 50 (basalt) | a lava moat (a `lava` floor, glowing `[r, g, b, glow]`), basalt columns | ambient 0.55; orange lava lights along the fence; 4 light towers | ash | minor drums, saw bass |\n| **Tranquility Dome** | Meteors | lunar night: the `space` preset, a big Earth disc, the dome's ribs drawn as arcs | 1350 / 1450 / 1550 / 1450 / 1350, h 60 | regolith with craters, habitat modules, antennas, a rover | ambient 0.55; cold blue-white floods | none (\"no air, no wind, no excuses\") | spacey arpeggios |\n| **Boneyard Ballpark** | Haunts | midnight: a full moon and fog layers | 1000 / 1080 / 1120 / 1080 / 1000, h 36 (an iron cemetery fence) | tombstones, crypts, dead trees, lanterns, candles | ambient 0.45; green and blue lanterns; jack-o'-lantern glow | `leaves` weather, plus fog as Backdrop `fog` layers | spooky organ, theremin |\n| **MegaMax MegaDome** | Mechanicals | neon night: the `city-night` preset seen through the dome's glass ribs, holo-billboards | 1000 / 1060 / 1100 / 1060 / 1000, h 40; the **Mega Wall** in left (θ −45° to −20°) is h 90 | a neon concourse floor (cyan grid lines), the luxury box, drones | ambient 0.6; magenta and cyan neon lights; the MEGA-TRON's glow | none (indoors) | synthwave |\n\n**Per-park physics** (in the park def): `gravityMul` (moon 0.55), `evMul` (moon 0.8), `jumpMul` (moon 2.0), `bounceMul`\n(dome turf 1.1), and the outfielders' depth shift (moon +200 U). **Floor materials:** grass with the park's mow pattern\n(Kernel stripes, Barnacle checkerboard, Caldera dark burnt grass, Tranquility bright astroturf, Boneyard dead brown-green,\nMegaDome turf with cyan neon lines).\n\n### 7.3 Pitch catalog (`11-pitch.js` data)\n\nThe heater's base speed is 340 U/s. Break is in U at the plate (+x is toward the glove side for a right-handed\npitcher); k is the lateness exponent.\n\n| Pitch | Speed × | Break x, z | k | Special |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Heater (fastball) | 1.00 | 0, +1.5 | 1.0 | a little \"rise\" |\n| Curve (\"Uncle Charlie\") | 0.74 | +7, −10 | 2.2 | |\n| Slider | 0.86 | +9, −3 | 2.0 | |\n| Changeup (\"the Pillow\") | 0.66 | −3, −6 | 1.6 | |\n| Sinker | 0.92 | −4, −6 | 1.8 | |\n| Knuckler | 0.60 | 0, −3 | – | wobble: noise of amplitude 4 at 3 Hz from the pitch's own seed |\n| *Signatures* | | | | Butterfly Knuckler (knuckler ×1.6 wobble, it flutters), Riptide Curve, Magma Sinker, Zero-G Floater, Phantom Forkball, Gigavolt Heater (§7.1) |\n| *Team supers* | ×1.35 unless stated | | | Corn Popper, Tidal Wave, Meteor Heater, Black Hole Ball, Banshee Ball, Overclock Laser (§7.1) |\n| Pitch-O-Matic (Derby) | 0.80 | small random breaks from `rng.deal` | – | 80% in the zone; every 5th is a Golden Ball |\n\nRepertoires: Ike (heater, curve, knuckler, Butterfly); Kid Kernel (heater, slider, changeup, signature = a 1.15×\n\"Kernel Cannon\" heater); each rival ace (heater, curve or slider, changeup or sinker, signature); relievers (heater,\nslider, changeup, the team's signature).\n\n### 7.4 Supers\n","numLines":400,"startLine":1100,"totalLines":2144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XSLdbQkQ8xsePW71nJRu2p","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
